add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Diagnostic Certainty in Acute Pericarditis',
 'A patient has characteristic new pleuritic positional chest pain and elevated CRP. ECG is normal, no rub is heard, and echo shows no effusion. CMR has not been performed. Acute coronary and pulmonary emergencies have been appropriately assessed. Under the ESC 2025 diagnostic-certainty framework, which conclusion is best?',
 'Possible pericarditis; additional assessment can establish greater diagnostic certainty',[
 ('Definite pericarditis is already established by pain and CRP','The 2025 framework counts CRP as an additional inflammatory criterion, but a definite clinical diagnosis requires more than one additional criterion beyond the compatible presentation. The supplied evidence provides only one.'),
 ('Pericarditis is rejected because ECG and echocardiography are normal','A normal ECG and absence of effusion do not exclude pericardial inflammation. These tests provide particular supportive findings rather than obligatory abnormalities in every affected patient.'),
 ('Myopericarditis is established by the inflammatory biomarker elevation','CRP indicates inflammation and is not a myocardial-injury biomarker. Myocardial involvement requires appropriate injury evidence, such as troponin elevation and contextual imaging, rather than CRP alone.')],
 [S('Count Findings Within the Named Framework','**The compatible presentation is considered separately from the additional diagnostic evidence.** ESC 2025 grades certainty instead of treating all suggestive chest pain as a proven diagnosis.',
    'The additional domains include a pericardial rub, widespread ST elevation or PR depression, CRP elevation, a new or worsening effusion, and imaging evidence of pericardial edema or enhancement.',
    'Do not count one ECG manifestation twice or convert a normal scan into positive evidence. A finding must be credible and interpreted after considering other causes.'),
  S('Use Uncertainty to Guide the Next Assessment','**Possible disease is a working assessment, not an instruction to dismiss symptoms or prescribe indefinite immunosuppression.** Repeat examination, inflammatory markers and selected CMR can clarify an unresolved presentation.',
    'First exclude a time-sensitive competing cause when indicated. Pericarditic pain can coexist with coronary disease, and an incidental effusion can have a noninflammatory cause.',
    'ACC 2025 also proposes compatible pain or an equivalent presentation plus additional findings, with one additional finding categorized as possible and two or more as definite. This differs from simply reciting the older two-of-four clinical rule. Identify the guideline year in an examination question. Troponin, ventricular function and rhythm answer the separate question of myocardial involvement and risk; a CRP result cannot replace them.')],['ESC2025','ACC2025'],
 table=(['ESC 2025 clinical certainty','Compatible presentation plus'],[
 ['Definite pericarditis','More than one additional criterion'],['Possible pericarditis','One additional criterion'],['Unlikely/rejected','No additional criterion; reassess alternatives and the clinical context']]))

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Pericardial ECG Changes and Coronary Mimics',
 'A hemodynamically stable adult with positional pleuritic pain has widespread concave ST elevation and PR depression. Which additional ECG finding would most strongly increase concern for a coronary occlusion rather than uncomplicated pericarditis?',
 'Reciprocal ST depression in aVL accompanying inferior ST elevation',[
 ('PR elevation in aVR','Reciprocal PR elevation in aVR can accompany atrial epicardial inflammation and diffuse PR depression in pericarditis. It does not carry the same implication as a territorial reciprocal ST pattern.'),
 ('ST depression limited to aVR and V1','These leads can show reciprocal ST depression in a diffuse pericardial pattern. Depression in other leads, especially a territorial reciprocal pattern, raises more concern for an ischemic explanation.'),
 ('Downsloping of the TP segment in multiple leads','Spodick sign can support pericarditis, although it is neither sensitive nor specific enough to establish the diagnosis. It is less concerning for coronary occlusion than the supplied reciprocal aVL change.')],
 [S('Interpret Distribution Rather Than Shape Alone','**Widespread ST elevation with PR displacement supports pericardial inflammation, whereas a vascular-territory pattern with reciprocal changes favors ischemia.** No isolated ECG sign safely excludes an acute coronary occlusion.',
    'Concave ST elevation can occur in early infarction. ST elevation in lead III exceeding lead II, reciprocal depression outside aVR or V1, evolving Q waves and corresponding regional dysfunction should increase ischemic concern.',
    'Early repolarization can produce stable concave ST elevation and J-point notching, but does not itself explain new inflammatory pain, a rub or a rising inflammatory marker. Prior tracings and serial change can help.'),
  S('Recognize the Limits of Classical Staging','**The traditional ST/PR, normalization, T-wave inversion and recovery sequence is not obligatory.** Treatment and timing can modify the observed ECG, and some patients never show the classical changes.',
    'PR depression is not exclusive to pericarditis, and Spodick sign has also been observed with STEMI. Use these as contextual clues rather than diagnostic vetoes.',
    'When symptoms or serial findings suggest ischemia, follow the urgent ACS pathway rather than waiting for a textbook pericarditis stage. Obtain troponin and echo as clinically indicated, recognizing that injury markers may rise in either infarction or inflammatory myocardial involvement. A normal or atypical tracing does not end the investigation when the clinical presentation remains concerning.')],['ECG2020','ESC2025'],
 table=(['Pattern','Interpretation'],[['Diffuse ST elevation with PR depression','Supports pericardial inflammation in the clinical context'],['Territorial ST elevation with reciprocal aVL depression','Raises concern for inferior coronary occlusion'],['Stable J-point notching on previous ECG','Supports a repolarization variant; does not explain new symptoms'],['TP downsloping','Supportive clue with limited specificity']]))

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'First-Line Echocardiography in Suspected Pericardial Disease',
 'Which imaging modality is usually first line in suspected pericarditis to assess effusion, hemodynamic effects and ventricular function?',
 'Transthoracic echocardiography',[
 ('Contrast-enhanced cardiac magnetic resonance','CMR is especially useful for tissue characterization, uncertainty, recurrence and suspected constriction or myocardial involvement. It does not replace the readily available initial functional and effusion assessment in a straightforward stable first presentation.'),
 ('ECG-gated contrast cardiac CT','CT defines anatomy, calcification, masses and selected alternative thoracic pathology. It is not the usual initial test for dynamic filling physiology and bedside ventricular assessment.'),
 ('Transesophageal echocardiography','TEE can identify posterior or loculated collections when transthoracic imaging is inadequate, particularly after surgery. Its semi-invasive nature makes it a selected complementary examination rather than the routine initial study here.')],
 [S('Define What the First Study Must Answer','**TTE assesses the amount and distribution of fluid, chamber function and evidence of impaired filling.** These findings guide immediate triage even though echocardiography does not directly measure every component of inflammation.',
    'Examine several views rather than measuring one echo-free stripe. Document whether an effusion is circumferential or loculated and whether there are concerning chamber-collapse or Doppler findings.',
    'Evaluate both ventricles and regional wall motion. A raised troponin with new ventricular dysfunction changes the concern for associated myocarditis or an alternative ischemic process.'),
  S('Do Not Confuse a Normal TTE With a Normal Pericardium','**Acute pericarditis can occur without an effusion or visible thickening.** A normal initial TTE therefore does not invalidate a clinical inflammatory presentation.',
    'CMR can demonstrate pericardial edema and enhancement when uncertainty persists or symptoms recur. CT is particularly useful for calcification and extracardiac anatomy; choose it for a specific question rather than as an interchangeable scan.',
    'Reassess promptly if hypotension, increasing breathlessness, syncope or venous congestion develops. A prior small effusion is not protection against subsequent enlargement or rapid accumulation. Conversely, an isolated stable small effusion does not prove active pericarditis. In technically difficult postoperative or regional compression cases, escalate to TEE or appropriate cross-sectional imaging rather than relying on a single reassuring anterior transthoracic view.')],['ASE2013','IMAGING2024','ACC2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'CMR Evidence of Pericardial Inflammation',
 'A stable patient with recurrent pericarditic pain has normal CRP and an equivocal echocardiogram. Appropriate assessment of competing emergencies is reassuring. The next question is whether active pericardial inflammation is present, and CMR is feasible. Which imaging test is most useful?',
 'CMR with edema-sensitive sequences and late gadolinium enhancement',[('Contrast-enhanced cardiac CT', 'CT is useful for calcification, detailed anatomy, masses and selected alternative thoracic causes, and can be an alternative when CMR is unavailable or unsuitable. Feasible CMR more directly combines edema-sensitive tissue assessment with pericardial enhancement for this inflammatory-activity question.'), ('Cardiac FDG-PET with whole-body assessment', 'PET has selected roles in unresolved inflammatory or malignant disease and can identify extracardiac targets. It is not the usual next test ahead of feasible CMR for focused pericardial tissue characterization in this presentation.'), ('Repeat echocardiography with myocardial strain analysis', 'Repeat echo and strain can clarify ventricular function or associated myocardial involvement. They do not provide the same direct pericardial edema and enhancement assessment when the initial echocardiogram leaves the tissue-activity question unresolved.')],
 [S('Separate Edema From Contrast Enhancement','**T2-sensitive edema and pericardial LGE provide complementary information.** Concordant abnormalities can strengthen an inflammatory interpretation when the symptoms and alternative diagnoses fit.',
    'The normal pericardium is thin, so spatial resolution, motion and adjacent fat or fluid can affect interpretation. An apparent bright line must be confirmed on appropriate sequences and views.',
    'Pericardial thickening and effusion add anatomical information but are not required for every active inflammatory episode. CT depicts calcification particularly well, while CMR adds tissue and dynamic functional assessment.'),
  S('Use Follow-Up Imaging for a Clinical Question','**Residual LGE is not identical to persisting edema or treatment failure.** Enhancement can diminish more slowly than pain or CRP, and prior surgery can complicate its interpretation.',
    'In a patient with normal CRP but recurrent convincing symptoms, imaging may reveal an inflammatory phenotype that a single blood test misses. Conversely, recurrent chest pain without objective evidence warrants reassessment for other causes.',
    'Serial imaging is most useful when it will change treatment, clarify refractory symptoms or assess potentially reversible constriction. Do not prescribe indefinite immunosuppression from one scan or require every mild first episode to undergo repeated CMR. Review ventricular function, myocardial tissue and rhythm separately if myocardial involvement is suspected. The practical aim is to establish the relevant tissue process and monitor an important uncertainty, rather than treating the word enhancement as a diagnosis, pathogen identification or fixed drug indication.')],['IMAGING2024','ACC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Admission and Etiological Evaluation in Acute Pericarditis',
 'A patient with a first pericarditis episode has a temperature of 38.7°C, a 24-mm effusion and preserved blood pressure. There is no chamber collapse. Which disposition is most appropriate?',
 'Hospital assessment with monitoring and a directed search for a specific cause',[
 ('Outpatient anti-inflammatory treatment with routine review in two weeks','Outpatient care is appropriate for uncomplicated low-risk disease with reliable follow-up. Fever above thirty-eight degrees and a large effusion are high-risk features even before hypotension or tamponade develops.'),
 ('Immediate pericardiocentesis based on the effusion measurement alone','Drainage is urgent for clinical tamponade and can be indicated for suspected purulent disease or important diagnostic or symptomatic reasons. A large measurement alone does not prove tamponade or make every stable patient require immediate puncture.'),
 ('Outpatient prednisone while awaiting a clinical response','Steroids can treat selected noninfectious causes or refractory disease. Starting them without assessment of this high-risk febrile effusion can obscure a treatable infection and does not provide adequate monitoring.')],
 [S('Recognize Red Flags Before Hemodynamic Collapse','**Risk assessment concerns both complications and an underlying nonidiopathic cause.** Preserved blood pressure does not erase the significance of a febrile large effusion.',
    'Major red flags include fever >38°C, subacute onset, a large effusion >20 mm, tamponade and failure to respond to aspirin or NSAIDs after at least one week.',
    'Myocardial involvement, immunodepression, trauma and oral anticoagulant treatment are additional contextual concerns. Clinical judgment also accounts for comorbidity, diagnostic uncertainty and access to follow-up.'),
  S('Investigate According to the Actual Risk','**Admission permits serial hemodynamic assessment and targeted investigation for causes that need specific treatment.** Consider TB, bacterial infection, malignancy and systemic inflammatory disease according to geography, exposure and phenotype.',
    'Obtain baseline ECG, echo, blood counts, inflammatory markers, renal function and appropriate injury biomarkers. Cultures, chest imaging, immune testing and fluid or tissue sampling should answer a clinical hypothesis.',
    'The prospective poor-prognosis study established the importance of fever, a subacute course, large effusion or tamponade and anti-inflammatory treatment failure; contemporary guidance retains these clinical triage principles. Do not turn admission into automatic drainage or an indiscriminate panel of viral antibodies. A patient suitable for outpatient care instead needs an explicit treatment plan, early review and instructions for new breathlessness, syncope or worsening pain. Escalation depends on the evolving syndrome rather than waiting for the full hypotension, muffled-heart-sound and venous-distension triad.')],['TRIAGE2007','ESC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Directed Etiological Testing in Pericarditis',
 'A 27-year-old has a first low-risk pericarditis episode, normal renal function and a reassuring history and examination. There is no large effusion, systemic illness, immunodepression, relevant travel or TB exposure. Which additional etiological testing strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Use baseline assessment and reserve further testing for specific clues or an atypical course',[
 ('Obtain a broad viral antibody panel to identify the causative cardiac virus','Seropositivity commonly reflects previous systemic exposure and does not establish pericardial infection. Such a panel rarely changes management of an uncomplicated low-risk presentation.'),
 ('Obtain pericardial tissue before classifying the episode as likely idiopathic','Biopsy has a role in selected unresolved, recurrent effusive or suspected malignant or infectious disease. Its invasive risk is not justified as a routine etiological requirement in this reassuring first episode.'),
 ('Obtain an extensive autoimmune antibody panel before beginning treatment','Immune testing is useful when systemic features, a relevant history or an atypical course suggest an autoimmune cause. Unselected broad testing can generate incidental results without identifying the cause of this episode.')],
 [S('Distinguish a Working Etiology From a Proven Pathogen','**Idiopathic or presumed viral pericarditis is a clinical working category after an appropriate evaluation.** It does not claim that a particular virus has been demonstrated in the pericardium.',
    'History should address infection, TB risk, cancer, kidney disease, medications, immune disease, cardiac injury and recent procedures. Review fever, constitutional symptoms, rash, arthritis and associated pulmonary or neurological findings.',
    'A baseline evaluation still includes ECG, inflammatory and hematological assessment, relevant renal testing and echocardiography. Troponin helps assess possible myocardial involvement when indicated.'),
  S('Escalate Testing When It Can Change Treatment','**A high-risk feature, recurrence or unexpected treatment failure should reopen the etiological assessment.** The useful investigation is the one that tests a plausible, actionable cause.',
    'For suspected bacterial disease, obtain cultures and appropriate fluid studies rather than a serum viral panel. In possible TB, consider local prevalence, extracardiac sites, fluid microbiology and tissue yield.',
    'A new malignant-appearing effusion, weight loss or a mass changes the imaging and sampling strategy. Systemic inflammatory clues justify focused immune investigation and specialist review. Absence of these features does not justify withholding initial anti-inflammatory treatment until every possible cause has been excluded. Conversely, the label idiopathic must not persist unquestioned when the course becomes febrile, progressive, recurrent or hemodynamically important. Explain this staged approach so reassessment is planned clinical care rather than a claim of absolute etiological certainty.')],['ESC2025','ACC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Myopericarditis and Predominant Myocardial Disease',
 'A patient meets pericarditis criteria and has elevated troponin. Echo and CMR show preserved regional and global ventricular function, without a new wall-motion abnormality. Which term best describes the conventional pericarditis-predominant overlap phenotype?',
 'Myopericarditis',[
 ('Perimyocarditis','This term conventionally describes predominant myocardial involvement, particularly when new ventricular dysfunction accompanies the pericardial syndrome. The supplied preserved function favors the pericarditis-predominant overlap term.'),
 ('Isolated acute pericarditis without myocardial injury','An inflammatory marker such as CRP may accompany isolated pericarditis, but the supplied troponin elevation requires assessment of myocardial injury. It cannot simply be omitted from the phenotype description.'),
 ('Inflammatory dilated cardiomyopathy','An inflammatory cardiomyopathy involves an appropriate myocardial structural-functional phenotype. Elevated troponin with preserved function in an acute pericardial syndrome does not establish ventricular dilatation or chronic inflammatory cardiomyopathy.')],
 [S('Describe the Dominant Clinical Phenotype','**Myopericarditis denotes pericarditis with evidence of myocardial injury but without newly impaired ventricular function in the conventional framework.** Perimyocarditis places greater emphasis on myocardial disease.',
    'The distinction uses imaging and function alongside biomarkers. Troponin concentration alone is not an EF measurement and cannot establish the severity or cause of ventricular impairment.',
    'Contemporary guidance also uses the broader inflammatory myopericardial syndrome framework. The terminology should clarify the actual findings rather than replace a description of function, arrhythmia and hemodynamic status.'),
  S('Assess Risk and Follow Both Components','**Exclude a relevant ischemic cause and evaluate electrical as well as mechanical risk.** Sustained arrhythmia, advanced AV block or worsening perfusion creates a different care requirement from uncomplicated chest pain.',
    'CMR can characterize myocardial edema and nonischemic injury when clinically indicated. A pericardial LGE line alone is not a myocardial criterion; conversely, preserved EF does not eliminate all myocardial arrhythmic risk.',
    'Treat the dominant pericardial pain/inflammatory syndrome appropriately when there is no HF contraindication, and plan follow-up and exercise advice that account for the myocardial component. Newly reduced EF requires the relevant myocardial-dysfunction pathway and can justify more intensive evaluation. Do not infer that every troponin-positive pericarditis requires an invasive biopsy, empiric antiviral therapy or pulse immunosuppression. The useful examination distinction is injury without new dysfunction versus predominant myocardial disease with functional consequences, followed by risk-based care rather than reassurance from terminology alone.')],['ESC2025','ACC2025'],
 table=(['Conventional term','Key distinction'],[['Myopericarditis','Pericarditis + myocardial injury; no newly impaired LV function'],['Perimyocarditis','Predominant myocarditis; new ventricular dysfunction may accompany pericardial findings'],['Isolated pericarditis','No established associated myocardial injury']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Initial Anti-Inflammatory Treatment of Acute Pericarditis',
 'An 82-kg adult has a first uncomplicated idiopathic pericarditis episode with persistent pain. Renal and hepatic function are normal, and there is no ulcer history, drug interaction or aspirin/NSAID intolerance. Which initial regimen best fits contemporary first-line treatment?',
 'Ibuprofen 600 mg every eight hours with colchicine and a proton pump inhibitor',[
 ('Ibuprofen 200 mg every eight hours with colchicine and a proton pump inhibitor','This may provide analgesia, but it is below the usual full anti-inflammatory ibuprofen regimen for active pericarditis. Persistent inflammatory pain calls for an adequate anti-inflammatory dose unless patient-specific toxicity limits it.'),
 ('Ibuprofen 600 mg every eight hours with a proton pump inhibitor','An appropriate NSAID dose treats pain and inflammation, but omission of colchicine loses an evidence-supported recurrence-prevention component. NSAID without colchicine is selected when colchicine is unsuitable, which this stem excludes.'),
 ('Prednisone 0.5 mg/kg/day with colchicine and a proton pump inhibitor','Corticosteroids have a role with specific indications or failure, intolerance or contraindication to first-line treatment. This uncomplicated eligible first episode does not require starting a steroid-based pathway.')],
 [S('Prescribe an Anti-Inflammatory Dose, Not Only an Analgesic','**First-line treatment combines aspirin or an NSAID with colchicine.** Add gastroprotection and review renal function, blood pressure, bleeding risk, interacting drugs and the clinical setting.',
    'ESC 2025 reference regimens include aspirin 750–1000 mg three times daily or ibuprofen 600–800 mg three times daily. Indomethacin 25–50 mg three times daily is another anti-inflammatory regimen, often used in incessant or recurrent disease.',
    'Aspirin is preferred with associated ischemic heart disease. Do not combine multiple NSAIDs to compensate for an inadequate response; assess diagnosis, adherence, dose and complications.'),
  S('Link Duration and Taper to Remission','**The usual initial full-dose aspirin/NSAID period is one to two weeks, with treatment individualized to response.** Maintain adequate treatment until pain and objective inflammatory activity have resolved before tapering.',
    'Reference taper steps are aspirin reductions of 250 mg or ibuprofen reductions of 200 mg at intervals of one to two weeks, adjusted to clinical response. These are decreases in the dose at each administration, not a new instruction to take the drug once daily.',
    'For this higher-weight patient with normal organ function, a common colchicine regimen is 0.5 mg twice daily for the first-episode course. Colchicine reduces recurrence risk and should not be stopped just because pain settles within days. Monitor gastrointestinal intolerance, renal function, blood count and other tests according to exposure and risk. Persistent fever, worsening breathlessness, a growing effusion or nonresponse after an adequate week requires reassessment rather than repeated unsupervised dose escalation.')],['ESC2025','ICAP'],
 table=(['Drug','Reference adult anti-inflammatory regimen'],[['Aspirin','750–1000 mg orally three times daily'],['Ibuprofen','600–800 mg orally three times daily'],['Indomethacin','25–50 mg orally three times daily'],['Colchicine adjunct','Weight-, organ-function- and interaction-adjusted; first episode generally at least 3 months']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Colchicine Dose, Duration and the ICAP Trial',
 'Which colchicine schedule was tested in ICAP for adults weighing more than 70 kg with a first acute pericarditis episode and suitable organ function?',
 'Colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for three months',[
 ('Colchicine 0.5 mg once daily for three months','The once-daily schedule was the ICAP lower-weight regimen and can also be appropriate when tolerance or organ function requires adjustment. The supplied patient exceeds the trial weight threshold and has no supplied reason for reduction.'),
 ('Colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for six months','A course of at least six months is commonly used for recurrent or incessant disease. The named ICAP trial tested three months in a first acute episode; longer recurrence protocols should not be substituted for that study design.'),
 ('Colchicine 1 mg twice on day one, then 0.5 mg twice daily for three months','Loading schedules have appeared in older colchicine regimens and differ from modern weight-adjusted pericarditis treatment. ICAP used the stated daily weight-adjusted dose without this loading schedule.')],
 [S('Apply the Studied Regimen to the Studied Patient','**ICAP added weight-adjusted colchicine to conventional aspirin or ibuprofen in first-episode acute pericarditis.** The question asks about the trial regimen rather than a prescription for every patient.',
    'The trial used 0.5 mg twice daily when weight was >70 kg and 0.5 mg once daily when weight was ≤70 kg, for three months. It did not use the proposed loading dose.',
    'Clinical prescribing must also account for tolerance, organ function and interacting drugs. The studied weight schedule is not a substitute for checking the product and the patient before continuing treatment.'),
  S('Understand What Colchicine Adds','**In ICAP, incessant or recurrent pericarditis occurred in 16.7% with colchicine versus 37.5% with placebo.** This was prevention of the composite inflammatory outcome, not proof of a mortality benefit.',
    'Colchicine binds tubulin and disrupts microtubule-dependent inflammatory functions, including neutrophil activity. It complements full-dose anti-inflammatory treatment rather than serving as a rapid analgesic loading treatment.',
    'Diarrhea and other gastrointestinal effects can limit use. More serious toxicity includes myopathy, marrow suppression and multiorgan injury, particularly with impaired clearance or interacting drugs. Check the complete medication list, including macrolides, azoles, antivirals, cyclosporine and other relevant inhibitors. Continue follow-up and assess recurrent symptoms objectively. A longer colchicine course may be required for a subsequent recurrent phenotype, while poor tolerance can require adjustment; neither situation changes what the original first-episode randomized trial actually tested.')],['ICAP','ESC2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Colchicine, Clarithromycin and Impaired Clearance',
 'A patient with stage 4 CKD takes colchicine for recurrent pericarditis. Clarithromycin is proposed for a new infection. Which medication decision best prevents a serious interaction?',
 'Avoid concomitant exposure by replacing clarithromycin or interrupting colchicine',[
 ('Continue both drugs after reducing colchicine to alternate-day dosing','Frequency reduction can be useful for selected less severe interactions in appropriate patients. It does not make a contraindicated strong-inhibitor combination safe in a patient with renal impairment.'),
 ('Separate the two drugs by twelve hours','Dose spacing can reduce some absorption interactions, but clarithromycin inhibits pathways involved in colchicine handling. This systemic interaction is not removed by separating administration times.'),
 ('Continue both drugs and use a normal baseline CK as the safety criterion','CK can help investigate myotoxicity, but a normal baseline result does not predict safety from subsequent accumulation. Waiting for a laboratory injury signal does not substitute for avoiding this high-risk combination.')],
 [S('Identify the Clearance Interaction','**Colchicine is a substrate of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein; clarithromycin inhibits both pathways.** Reduced clearance can produce major exposure increases, especially with renal or hepatic impairment.',
    'Current colchicine tablet labeling contraindicates use with P-gp or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors in patients with renal or hepatic impairment. Do not extrapolate a dose-reduction strategy intended for a different organ-function or inhibitor context.',
    'Other important interacting drugs include certain azoles, protease-inhibitor regimens and cyclosporine. Verapamil and diltiazem also require review, and concomitant myotoxic drugs can increase concern.'),
  S('Recognize Toxicity Before It Becomes Multiorgan Injury','**Early gastrointestinal toxicity can precede neuromuscular, hematological and systemic manifestations.** Severe diarrhea or vomiting in this exposure setting should prompt an interaction and overdose assessment.',
    'Look for proximal weakness, myalgia, CK elevation, cytopenias, hepatic abnormalities and worsening organ function. Stop the unsafe exposure and obtain urgent specialist or toxicology input when toxicity is suspected.',
    'Choose antimicrobial therapy according to the infection, likely organism and patient factors; avoiding clarithromycin must still leave an effective infection plan. If a potent interacting agent is essential, coordinate interruption and resumption rather than independently reducing tablets and assuming safety. Review all prescribed and nonprescribed medicines, renal trend and formulation. Pericarditis prevention remains important, but its benefit does not justify an avoidable potentially fatal pharmacokinetic interaction. A lower maintenance dose, lack of prior side effects or normal initial CK is not protection against accumulation after a new inhibitor is added.')],['COLCHLABEL','ESC2025'])

R['ACS2025']=('ACC/AHA acute coronary syndromes guideline, 2025: post-MI pericarditis','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001309')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Pericarditis After Myocardial Infarction',
 'Several days after a reperfused MI, a patient develops persistent pleuritic positional pain and a rub. Reinfarction and mechanical complications have been assessed. The patient receives aspirin and clopidogrel, with no major bleeding or renal contraindication. If anti-inflammatory medication is needed, which strategy is preferred?',
 'Use an aspirin-based anti-inflammatory regimen and consider adjunctive colchicine',[
 ('Replace aspirin temporarily with an ibuprofen-based anti-inflammatory regimen','Ibuprofen is a standard option in uncomplicated nonischemic pericarditis. After MI, aspirin is preferred; nonaspirin NSAIDs may adversely affect healing or recurrent ischemic risk and should not replace the antiplatelet plan.'),
 ('Use indomethacin as the preferred initial anti-inflammatory agent','Indomethacin can be used in selected nonischemic pericarditis settings. It is not preferred after MI because nonaspirin NSAID exposure can be harmful in this context.'),
 ('Use a prednisone-based regimen as the preferred initial approach','Corticosteroids are appropriate for selected specific causes or when other treatments cannot be used. They are not the preferred initial post-MI choice because of healing and recurrence concerns.')],
 [S('Reassess New Pain Before Labeling It Inflammatory','**Post-MI pain requires assessment for recurrent ischemia and structural complications.** A positional component and rub support pericarditis but do not independently exclude a second urgent process.',
    'Echo should assess function and effusion. A new large or hemorrhagic effusion, hypotension or an unexpected clinical deterioration raises concern for a mechanical complication rather than an uncomplicated pain syndrome.',
    'Early infarct-associated pericarditis differs in timing from a delayed immune-mediated post-cardiac injury syndrome. Both require the diagnosis to fit the overall course.'),
  S('Prefer Aspirin When Treatment Is Needed','**The 2025 ACS guideline supports high-dose aspirin for persistent symptomatic early or late post-MI pericarditis and consideration of colchicine.** Its reference aspirin regimen is 500–1000 mg every six to eight hours until symptoms improve.',
    'Coordinate the anti-inflammatory dose with the complete antiplatelet regimen, gastrointestinal protection and bleeding risk. Do not add a second aspirin prescription without accounting for what the patient already receives; other P2Y12 regimens can introduce additional prescribing constraints.',
    'The colchicine regimen should be adjusted to weight, organ function and interactions and continued for the appropriate inflammatory course. Evidence specifically dedicated to post-MI pericarditis is more limited than the general acute-pericarditis trial evidence. Nonaspirin NSAIDs and glucocorticoids are potentially harmful after MI because of recurrent ischemic or impaired healing concerns. An asymptomatic effusion by itself does not require routine high-dose aspirin or colchicine. If symptoms persist, reassess diagnosis and complications instead of merely escalating anti-inflammatory exposure.')],['ACS2025','ESC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Selecting Corticosteroids for a Specific Pericarditis Setting',
 'A patient has active lupus-associated pericarditis without shock, myocardial dysfunction or another organ-threatening lupus manifestation. A recent major ulcer bleed makes NSAID treatment unsuitable. Infection has been appropriately evaluated, and colchicine can be used. Which anti-inflammatory strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Prednisone 0.3 mg/kg/day with colchicine',[('Prednisone 1 mg/kg/day with colchicine', 'Higher doses can be needed for specific severe systemic indications. This stable isolated pericardial syndrome is better matched to the lower pericarditis range; higher exposure increases toxicity and can complicate withdrawal.'), ('Intravenous methylprednisolone 1 g daily for three days with colchicine', 'Pulse therapy has roles in life-threatening immune-mediated myocardial or systemic disease. The supplied stable isolated pericardial presentation usually permits an oral low-to-moderate dose strategy.'), ('Azathioprine with colchicine', 'Azathioprine can be a steroid-sparing treatment for selected systemic or refractory disease. Its delayed effect makes it less suitable than an effective oral glucocorticoid for prompt control of this acute episode.')],
 [S('Identify a Reason to Use Steroids','**A specific immune-mediated cause and an important aspirin/NSAID contraindication can justify corticosteroids.** This is different from starting steroids in every uncomplicated first idiopathic episode.',
    'Colchicine remains useful when it can be given safely. Address the systemic disease with the relevant specialist rather than treating pericardial symptoms as separate from all other lupus activity.',
    'Evaluate infection before immunosuppression, especially TB or bacterial disease when risk or features suggest it. A previous ulcer bleed requires a bleeding and gastroprotection plan rather than assuming any alternative anti-inflammatory is harmless.'),
  S('Use the Lowest Effective Dose With a Withdrawal Plan','**ESC 2025 lists prednisone 0.2–0.5 mg/kg/day as a low-to-moderate starting range for selected pericarditis patients.** Maintain initial treatment until clinical and objective inflammatory remission, then taper gradually.',
    'An initial two-to-four-week reference period does not mean the drug can be stopped abruptly at that point. The full taper often takes longer and must account for recurrence, total exposure and adrenal suppression.',
    'Monitor blood pressure, glucose, infection, weight, mood and bone risk, and provide appropriate prevention for prolonged exposure. Repeated high-dose rescue courses can create steroid dependence while leaving the disease poorly controlled. Persistent or recurrent inflammation despite an adequate plan should trigger reassessment of etiology and consideration of a steroid-sparing strategy matched to the phenotype. The aim is to control a justified indication with a feasible taper, not to regard the fastest short-term pain response as evidence that a higher dose is superior.')],['ESC2025','ACC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Slow Prednisone Taper at the Recurrence-Prone Range',
 'A patient with pericarditis has taken prednisone for several months and is now well on 12.5 mg/day with colchicine. CRP is normal. Previous flares occurred during rapid reductions below 15 mg/day. Which proposed next taper step is most consistent with the ESC 2025 low-dose approach?',
 'Reduce by 1.25 mg/day after about four weeks if remission persists',[
 ('Reduce by 2.5 mg/day every week','Smaller reductions can be appropriate, but the proposed weekly interval is faster than the low-dose reference schedule for a recurrence-prone patient. The vulnerable range calls for slower reassessment-guided reduction.'),
 ('Reduce by 5 mg/day every two weeks','Larger reductions are used at higher prednisone doses. At twelve-and-a-half milligrams daily, this represents a substantial proportional withdrawal and is poorly matched to the supplied relapse history.'),
 ('Change to 12.5 mg on alternate days immediately','Alternate-day approaches have selected uses in other long-term steroid settings. Abruptly halving average exposure is not equivalent to a gradual low-dose pericarditis taper and may trigger a flare or withdrawal symptoms.')],
 [S('Reduce More Slowly as the Dose Becomes Small','**Below prednisone 15 mg/day, ESC 2025 suggests reductions of 1.25–2.5 mg/day at intervals of two to six weeks.** The chosen step depends on remission durability and previous response.',
    'At higher doses the reference schedule permits larger changes: above fifty milligrams, ten-milligram reductions every one to two weeks; from fifty to twenty-five, five-to-ten-milligram reductions at similar intervals; from twenty-five to fifteen, two-and-a-half milligrams every two to four weeks.',
    'These are changes in the daily dose after the interval has elapsed, not instructions to reduce by that amount every day. Explicit prescription wording prevents a potentially serious taper misunderstanding.'),
  S('Confirm Remission and Distinguish a Flare From Withdrawal','**Each decrease should follow sustained symptom control and reassuring inflammatory assessment.** A new pain episode requires clinical review rather than automatic attribution to recurrent pericarditis.',
    'Check for objective inflammation and reconsider ischemic, musculoskeletal and other explanations. Steroid withdrawal or adrenal insufficiency can cause systemic symptoms and requires a different assessment from a true inflammatory relapse.',
    'Continue appropriate colchicine and avoid reducing multiple anti-inflammatory components simultaneously without a reasoned plan. If repeated attempts provoke documented inflammatory flares, reassess adherence, dose, cause and suitability for steroid-sparing treatment. Monitor cumulative steroid toxicity and provide bone and metabolic protection according to risk. The safe objective is eventual withdrawal with disease control, not indefinite maintenance simply because stopping too quickly previously failed. Taper instructions should state the current dose, next dose, duration, reassessment criteria and what to do if symptoms return.')],['ESC2025'],
 table=(['Current prednisone dose','ESC 2025 reference decrease'],[['>50 mg/day','10 mg/day every 1–2 weeks'],['50–25 mg/day','5–10 mg/day every 1–2 weeks'],['25–15 mg/day','2.5 mg/day every 2–4 weeks'],['<15 mg/day','1.25–2.5 mg/day every 2–6 weeks']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Return to Activity After Isolated Pericarditis',
 'Ten days after an uncomplicated isolated pericarditis episode, a runner is pain-free and CRP has normalized. Troponin and ventricular function were normal, and there is no arrhythmia or effusion. The patient requests immediate return to hard interval training. Which plan best reflects ACC 2025 guidance?',
 'Continue exercise restriction for at least one month and confirm remission before a graded return',[('Return to intense training now after confirming symptom and CRP resolution', 'Symptom and biomarker recovery are reassuring, but ten days is shorter than the named guidance minimum. Reassess for a graded return after the appropriate restriction and remission assessment.'), ('Resume intense training when the initial two-week NSAID course ends', 'The drug course and activity restriction answer different clinical questions. Completing a prescription does not independently establish readiness for intense exercise, and the proposed interval is shorter than the named recommendation.'), ('Continue restriction for six months before reassessing exercise clearance', 'Longer restrictions can be needed with myocardial inflammation, arrhythmia or persistent disease. The supplied isolated uncomplicated pericarditis phenotype does not require applying a high-risk myocardial-disease schedule by default.')],
 [S('Avoid Exercise During Active Pericardial Inflammation','**Exercise advice should incorporate the disease phenotype and objective remission.** ACC 2025 recommends restriction for at least one month after diagnosis or a flare, with activity-related heart rate kept below 100 beats/min until remission.',
    'Clinical remission integrates symptoms, examination, inflammatory markers and relevant ECG or echo findings. A normal pain score achieved while taking an analgesic is not by itself proof of resolved inflammation.',
    'The minimum interval is not a guarantee of clearance. Persistent symptoms, effusion, CRP elevation or a more complex course can require longer restriction and additional assessment.'),
  S('Reintroduce Activity According to the Recovery Assessment','**Isolated pericarditis differs from myocarditis with residual scar or ventricular arrhythmia.** Troponin elevation, new dysfunction, palpitations or syncope should prompt evaluation of an associated myocardial component.',
    'Once the relevant remission criteria are met, use a graded increase and a clear plan for recurrent pain or exercise intolerance. The return decision is separate from the duration of colchicine, which continues to reduce recurrence risk.',
    'Routine repeated CMR until every trace of enhancement disappears is not a universal clearance requirement for a straightforward recovered first episode. Selected imaging is appropriate for an unresolved clinical question, recurrent symptoms or suspected complications. Explain the difference between ordinary low-intensity daily activity and maximal interval or competitive endurance exercise. Review the patient rather than allowing an arbitrary date, the last NSAID tablet or one reassuring laboratory result to serve as the entire clearance assessment.')],['ACC2025','ESC2025'])

R['ACRPREG2020']=('ACR reproductive-health guideline, 2020: colchicine compatibility','https://doi.org/10.1002/art.41191')
R['FDANSAID']=('FDA safety communication on NSAIDs from twenty weeks of pregnancy','https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-recommends-avoiding-use-nsaids-pregnancy-20-weeks-or-later-because-they-can-result-low-amniotic')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Active Pericarditis in Late Pregnancy',
 'At 32 weeks of pregnancy, a patient already receiving tolerated colchicine for recurrent pericarditis develops a confirmed inflammatory flare with persistent pain. There is no infection, myocardial dysfunction or hemodynamic compromise. Which added anti-inflammatory strategy is most appropriate through a pregnancy-heart team?',
 'Use the lowest effective prednisone or prednisolone dose while reviewing continued colchicine',[
 ('Add ibuprofen 600 mg every eight hours','Ibuprofen can treat pericarditis outside pregnancy and in selected earlier-pregnancy settings. At thirty-two weeks, NSAID-related fetal renal and ductal risks make this an unsuitable routine full-dose plan.'),
 ('Add aspirin 750 mg every eight hours','Anti-inflammatory aspirin doses are different from low-dose obstetric or antiplatelet aspirin. The low-dose safety exception cannot be transferred to this high-dose late-pregnancy regimen.'),
 ('Add acetaminophen as the sole new treatment for the inflammatory flare','Acetaminophen can provide analgesia in pregnancy. It does not supply the needed anti-inflammatory disease control for the confirmed persistently symptomatic flare described here.')],
 [S('Match the Drug to Gestational Age','**Late pregnancy changes the aspirin/NSAID balance.** Around twenty weeks onward, NSAIDs can impair fetal renal function and produce oligohydramnios; at approximately thirty weeks and later, premature ductal closure is an additional concern.',
    'FDA advises limiting necessary use between twenty and thirty weeks to the lowest effective dose and shortest duration, and avoiding it from about thirty weeks. These cautions do not mean that all pain or pericardial inflammation should be left untreated.',
    'Low-dose aspirin for an obstetric or vascular indication is a distinct regimen. Do not equate it with the high doses used for acute inflammatory pericarditis.'),
  S('Preserve Disease Control With a Pregnancy-Compatible Plan','**Prednisone or prednisolone can be used at the minimal effective dose for active pericarditis during pregnancy.** ESC 2025 preferably limits prednisone to up to 20 mg/day when feasible; this is a treatment aim, not a reason to leave severe disease uncontrolled. Monitor maternal pressure, glucose and infection risk and plan tapering after remission.',
    'ESC 2025 allows consideration of colchicine during pregnancy, particularly for patients already using it to prevent recurrences. ACR reproductive guidance regards colchicine as compatible throughout pregnancy; it should not be reflexively discontinued solely because of pregnancy.',
    'Review renal and hepatic function, interactions, prior tolerance and the actual inflammatory phenotype before continuing or changing therapy. Lactation decisions require a drug-specific review after delivery rather than assuming every pregnancy restriction persists unchanged. Refractory disease may require specialist discussion of additional therapy, but a generic biologic or cytotoxic prescription is not the next default for this stable flare. Coordinate obstetric and cardiac monitoring and reassess promptly if breathlessness, syncope, hypotension or a growing effusion develops.')],['ESC2025','ACRPREG2020','FDANSAID'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Recurrent Versus Incessant Pericarditis',
 'A patient has pericarditic pain and objective inflammation continuing for eight weeks after the initial episode. Symptoms have fluctuated, but there has been no remission or symptom-free interval. Which course classification is most appropriate?',
 'Incessant pericarditis',[
 ('Recurrent pericarditis','Recurrence requires a new inflammatory episode after a documented symptom-free interval, conventionally at least four to six weeks. Fluctuation within continuously active disease does not supply that interval.'),
 ('Chronic pericarditis','The chronic label generally refers to disease lasting more than three months. Eight weeks of continuous activity fits the intermediate incessant course rather than this duration category.'),
 ('A new acute pericarditis episode','A newly acute event follows the relevant clinical history and time course. Continued inflammation without remission is not reset to a separate acute episode each time the pain becomes more intense.')],
 [S('Document Whether Remission Actually Occurred','**The distinction between incessant and recurrent disease depends on the intervening course, not merely the number of pain exacerbations.** Ask about symptom control, inflammatory markers and the treatment timeline.',
    'In the ACC 2025 duration scheme, an acute episode lasts less than roughly four to six weeks, an incessant episode persists beyond that period without remission, and chronic disease lasts more than three months.',
    'A recurrence follows a symptom-free interval of about four to six weeks or longer and should have evidence supporting renewed pericardial inflammation. ESC 2025 frames acute onset as up to four weeks and explicitly includes persistent symptoms or flares during ongoing treatment or drug taper within the incessant course. Its recurrent category requires a symptom-free interval, complete discontinuation of anti-inflammatory treatment and a subsequent supported episode. These distinctions differ from simply counting each on-treatment flare as a new recurrence.'),
  S('Use the Course to Reassess Management','**An incessant course warrants review of the original diagnosis, cause and adequacy of therapy.** It is not proof of persistent viral replication or an automatic indication for antibiotics.',
    'Review adherence, anti-inflammatory dose, colchicine exposure, steroid initiation and taper, drug intolerance and the possibility of a specific infectious, immune or malignant cause.',
    'Persistent pain alone can have an alternative mechanism. CRP, ECG, echo and selected CMR provide complementary objective information, especially before escalating immunosuppression. Incessant or recurrent disease often needs a longer treatment and taper plan than a straightforward first episode. New effusion, fever, myocardial dysfunction or hemodynamic change requires a fresh risk assessment. Accurate course documentation helps avoid both undertreating continuous inflammation and repeatedly labeling noninflammatory chest pain as a new recurrence.')],['ACC2025','ESC2025'],
 table=(['Conventional/ACC course','Distinguishing feature'],[['Acute','Initial short-duration inflammatory episode; exact boundary depends on the named scheme'],['Incessant','Persistent activity beyond approximately 4–6 weeks without remission'],['Recurrent','New supported inflammatory episode after a symptom-free interval of about 4–6 weeks or longer'],['Chronic','Disease persisting beyond 3 months']]))

R['CORP']=('CORP randomized colchicine trial in first recurrent pericarditis, 2011','https://doi.org/10.7326/0003-4819-155-7-201110040-00359')
R['CORP2']=('CORP-2 randomized colchicine trial in multiple pericarditis recurrences, 2014','https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(13)62709-9')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Treating a First Documented Recurrence',
 'After completing treatment, an 80-kg patient remains symptom-free for eight weeks and then develops confirmed recurrent idiopathic pericarditis. Renal and hepatic function remain normal and first-line drugs are tolerated. Which regimen is most appropriate?',
 'Restart full-dose aspirin or an NSAID and use colchicine for at least six months',[
 ('Restart full-dose aspirin or an NSAID and give colchicine for two weeks','Aspirin or NSAIDs address the active flare, but a short colchicine course does not reproduce evidence-supported recurrence prevention. Recurrent disease generally requires at least six months of colchicine.'),
 ('Use colchicine alone and defer an anti-inflammatory until the next recurrence','Colchicine reduces recurrence risk but is not a substitute for adequate treatment of the current painful inflammatory flare in a patient able to take aspirin or an NSAID.'),
 ('Begin prednisone plus colchicine instead of retrying aspirin or an NSAID','Steroids can be needed for specific causes or unsuitable first-line therapy. A first recurrence with tolerated aspirin/NSAID and no supplied contraindication does not automatically establish a reason to switch to steroids.')],
 [S('Treat the Flare and Prevent Further Episodes','**Recurrent pericarditis generally uses colchicine with an adequate anti-inflammatory regimen and a slower individualized withdrawal plan.** Confirm that the new episode represents inflammation rather than another cause of pain.',
    'Common adult regimens with normal clearance include ibuprofen 600–800 mg three times daily or aspirin 750–1000 mg three times daily, with gastroprotection. For this higher-weight patient, colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily is a usual reference regimen.',
    'Continue colchicine for at least six months in recurrent or incessant disease; the final duration can be longer according to remission and recurrence history. Assess interactions and organ function again rather than copying an old prescription without review.'),
  S('Relate Colchicine Evidence to the Clinical Course','**CORP studied a first recurrence, whereas CORP-2 studied multiple recurrences.** These trials support extending colchicine use beyond the initial-episode setting.',
    'CORP reported recurrence at eighteen months in 24% with colchicine versus 55% with placebo. CORP-2 enrolled patients with at least two recurrences and reported further recurrence in 21.6% versus 42.5% with six months of colchicine or placebo added to conventional treatment.',
    'Trial schedules are evidence sources, not instructions to reproduce older loading doses or ignore renal impairment. Repeated recurrences or first-line treatment failure justify renewed etiological and inflammatory-phenotype assessment. If corticosteroids become necessary, avoid high-dose dependence and plan tapering after remission. An IL-1 strategy can be appropriate in selected resistant inflammatory disease, but it is not required merely because one recurrence occurred after a previously successful first-line course.')],['CORP','CORP2','ESC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'An Inflammatory Flare During Prednisone Taper',
 'A patient on colchicine develops returning positional pain and CRP elevation while prednisone is reduced from 10 to 7.5 mg/day. There is no large effusion, myocardial dysfunction or contraindication to ibuprofen. Infection and adherence have been reviewed. Which next treatment strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Add full-dose ibuprofen, maintain colchicine and reassess the prednisone taper',[('Increase prednisone to 20 mg/day while continuing colchicine', 'Restoring steroid exposure can control inflammation when a suitable alternative is unavailable or a specific systemic indication requires it. Here adequate NSAID treatment is still available; using it avoids defaulting to another cycle of increased steroid exposure.'), ('Add azathioprine while continuing the present prednisone taper', 'Azathioprine can provide longer-term steroid-sparing benefit in selected refractory disease. Its delayed onset does not make it the preferred immediate response to an active flare, and the ongoing taper should be reassessed until remission returns.'), ('Replace the current regimen with daily anakinra', 'Anakinra is evidence-supported in selected resistant inflammatory recurrent or incessant disease. This supplied stable flare has an available first-line NSAID option and does not establish that adequate conventional treatment has failed.')],
 [S('Confirm the Flare and Review What Has Changed','**A documented inflammatory flare during steroid reduction should trigger a treatment review rather than an automatic high-dose steroid cycle.** New pain requires examination and exclusion of another important cause. ESC 2025 includes this on-treatment or taper-associated persistence/flare within its incessant course terminology, rather than requiring it to be called a fully off-treatment recurrence.',
    'Review colchicine adherence, dose, interactions, inflammatory markers and effusion or myocardial findings. Reconsider a specific etiology if the pattern is unexpectedly persistent or progressive.',
    'If an NSAID is suitable, adding adequate aspirin or NSAID treatment can regain control without escalating prednisone. A normal-renal-function adult reference is ibuprofen 600–800 mg orally every eight hours with proton pump inhibitor gastroprotection. Continue weight- and clearance-adjusted colchicine, pause further steroid reduction until remission and monitor renal and gastrointestinal safety.'),
  S('Break the Cycle of Repeated Steroid Rescue','**The vulnerable low-dose range often needs slower tapering after stable remission.** A patient repeatedly flaring at the same dose may need a phenotype-directed steroid-sparing strategy.',
    'IL-1 treatment is evidence-supported in selected refractory inflammatory recurrent disease, particularly with steroid dependence. A single stable flare still requires individualized assessment rather than treating a biologic as the only available next step.',
    'Avoid reducing several effective drugs simultaneously while inflammation remains active. Monitor cumulative steroid harms and possible adrenal suppression and distinguish these from true inflammatory recurrence. Once remission is restored, document the sequence and intervals of withdrawal, with particularly small prednisone reductions below fifteen milligrams daily. Escalating to a severe-disease immunosuppressive regimen is justified by the relevant disease indication, not merely by frustration with a taper. The plan should balance short-term flare control with a feasible route away from chronic steroid exposure.')],['ESC2025','ACC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'IL-1 Treatment When CRP Is Normal',
 'A patient has multiple well-documented pericarditis recurrences, colchicine resistance and corticosteroid dependence. During the current symptomatic episode, CRP is normal but CMR shows convincing pericardial edema and enhancement. First-line and steroid strategies have failed or become intolerable. Which interpretation best reflects ESC 2025 guidance?',
 'CMR-supported inflammation can justify considering an IL-1 agent despite a normal CRP',[('Defer IL-1 treatment until a subsequent CRP measurement is elevated', 'CRP can fail to capture pericardial inflammation, including with treatment or a localized process. The 2025 guidance permits consideration based on convincing CMR inflammation in the relevant resistant clinical setting.'), ('Obtain a pericardial biopsy to document inflammation before considering IL-1 treatment', 'Biopsy can answer a specific unresolved infectious or malignant etiological question. It is not a routine prerequisite when the established resistant inflammatory phenotype is convincingly supported by CMR as supplied here.'), ('Use azathioprine before IL-1 treatment because CRP is normal', 'Azathioprine has selected steroid-sparing uses, but it is not an obligatory intermediate step in the named CMR-supported resistant inflammatory setting. Drug choice depends on cause, phenotype, response and safety.')],
 [S('Recognize That Blood and Tissue Findings Are Complementary','**The inflammatory phenotype is not defined by CRP alone.** Credible edema and enhancement can provide objective support when the clinical history and disease course fit.',
    'Exclude alternative chest-pain causes, scan artefact and residual nonactive enhancement. The supplied edema and repeatedly documented inflammatory history make this more persuasive than an isolated incidental LGE finding.',
    'Review ongoing anti-inflammatory exposure, because a measured CRP can be influenced by treatment. A single normal result is not a substitute for longitudinal assessment.'),
  S('Match Escalation to the Guideline and Patient','**ESC 2025 distinguishes a strong recommendation in resistant recurrent disease with elevated CRP from consideration in selected CMR-inflamed cases regardless of CRP.** The latter is a conditional expert-guidance pathway, not identical trial evidence in every subgroup.',
    'Anakinra blocks the IL-1 receptor; rilonacept is a soluble trap for IL-1α and IL-1β. Adult reference regimens are anakinra 1–2 mg/kg/day subcutaneously, maximum 100 mg/day, or rilonacept 320 mg subcutaneously once followed by 160 mg weekly. Review anakinra renal clearance and neutrophil counts, rilonacept lipids, infection risk, vaccination and the drug-specific withdrawal plan. Regulatory indications vary by jurisdiction.',
    'IL-1 therapy can control inflammation and permit corticosteroid withdrawal, but it does not treat every specific infection, malignancy or noninflammatory pain syndrome. Assess TB and other infection risk and review vaccination before initiation. Reassess symptoms, inflammatory activity and relevant imaging after treatment rather than defining success solely by a CRP that was already normal. Do not convert the CRP-normal allowance into a claim that every patient with recurrent pain needs a biologic or that the drug is curative after a fixed number of injections.')],['ESC2025','IMAGING2024','ACC2025','RILOLABEL','ANAKLABEL'])

R['RHAPSODY']=('RHAPSODY randomized-withdrawal rilonacept trial, 2021','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2027892')
R['AIRTRIP']=('AIRTRIP randomized-withdrawal anakinra trial, 2016','https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.15826')
R['RILOLABEL']=('Rilonacept current US prescribing information, revised 2025','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/125249Orig1s057lbl.pdf')
R['ANAKLABEL']=('Anakinra current US prescribing information','https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=d9d74915-6606-4570-9c52-c4001d3177de')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'RHAPSODY: Interpreting a Randomized-Withdrawal Trial',
 'Which statement correctly describes the randomized comparison in RHAPSODY?',
 'Patients responding during rilonacept run-in were randomized to continued treatment or placebo withdrawal',[
 ('Randomization occurred before participants first received rilonacept','A conventional parallel initiation trial would randomize before drug exposure. RHAPSODY first used an active run-in, then randomized qualifying responders, which matters when interpreting treatment effects.'),
 ('The trial directly compared rilonacept with anakinra','Both agents have pericarditis evidence, but RHAPSODY compared continued rilonacept with placebo withdrawal. Separate trials do not establish their head-to-head relative efficacy.'),
 ('The randomized primary endpoint was pain resolution during the initial run-in','Initial symptom and CRP improvement were important run-in observations. The randomized primary endpoint concerned time to first adjudicated recurrence, a different phase and outcome.')],
 [S('Understand the Enriched Study Population','**RHAPSODY enrolled symptomatic recurrent pericarditis with systemic inflammation and used active rilonacept before withdrawal randomization.** Background oral therapies were withdrawn during the run-in under the study protocol.',
    'Of eighty-six patients entering run-in, sixty-one underwent randomization. The comparison therefore estimates maintenance benefit in qualifying responders rather than the effect of starting the drug in every person with chest pain.',
    'Rilonacept is a soluble IL-1 trap that binds IL-1α and IL-1β. This mechanism differs from anakinra, which blocks the IL-1 receptor, and from a selective IL-1β antibody.'),
  S('Interpret Recurrence Reduction Without Expanding the Claim','**During randomized withdrawal, recurrence occurred in 2/30 patients receiving rilonacept and 23/31 receiving placebo.** The reported hazard ratio was 0.04, with 95% CI 0.01–0.18.',
    'These results support a major reduction in recurrence while treatment continues in the studied population. They do not prove a mortality benefit, permanent cure after withdrawal or superiority to anakinra.',
    'The usual adult labeled regimen is a single 320-mg loading dose followed by 160 mg weekly by subcutaneous injection. The loading dose is not repeated daily. Evaluate active infection, TB risk and vaccines before use; monitor infection, injection-site reactions and lipids. A strong trial result must still be applied to a confirmed inflammatory recurrent phenotype with an appropriate safety and follow-up plan. Discuss what will be reassessed during treatment and how eventual withdrawal will be considered, because suppression of symptoms does not establish that the underlying recurrence tendency has disappeared.')],['RHAPSODY','RILOLABEL'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'AIRTRIP and Anakinra in Resistant Recurrent Pericarditis',
 'Which agent was directly studied in AIRTRIP for colchicine-resistant, corticosteroid-dependent recurrent idiopathic pericarditis?',
 'Anakinra',[
 ('Rilonacept','Rilonacept is an IL-1 trap with randomized recurrent-pericarditis evidence from RHAPSODY. It was not the study drug in AIRTRIP, despite a related clinical application.'),
 ('Canakinumab','Canakinumab selectively neutralizes IL-1β and is used in defined autoinflammatory conditions. It does not have the AIRTRIP trial evidence, and related cytokine targets do not make agents interchangeable.'),
 ('Azathioprine','Azathioprine is a purine-antimetabolite immunosuppressant used in selected steroid-sparing settings. AIRTRIP tested an IL-1 receptor antagonist rather than this slower-acting strategy.')],
 [S('Identify the Studied Resistant Phenotype','**AIRTRIP was a small randomized-withdrawal study of anakinra in highly selected inflammatory recurrent disease.** Participants had colchicine resistance, corticosteroid dependence, repeated recurrences and elevated CRP.',
    'Twenty-one patients initially received anakinra for two months. Responders were then assigned to continued anakinra or placebo withdrawal for the subsequent study period.',
    'The trial used anakinra 2 mg/kg/day, up to 100 mg/day, by subcutaneous injection. Contemporary specialist regimens often use the adult maximum daily dose with individual safety adjustment.'),
  S('Connect Mechanism, Benefit and Monitoring','**Anakinra is a recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist that blocks signaling from IL-1α and IL-1β.** In AIRTRIP, recurrent pericarditis occurred in 2/11 patients continuing anakinra versus 9/10 receiving placebo.',
    'The small sample and withdrawal design limit broad comparisons. The result supports a steroid-sparing option in the selected phenotype, not automatic treatment of a first uncomplicated episode or proof of superiority to rilonacept.',
    'Injection-site reactions are common, and infection and neutropenia require attention. Obtain baseline blood count, renal function and infection assessment and review vaccination. The US label recommends neutrophil monitoring before treatment, monthly for three months and then quarterly up to one year. Severe renal impairment affects clearance; a reduced dosing frequency is considered in labeled renal guidance and must be adapted by the treating specialist for the pericarditis context. Pericarditis use and regulatory authorization vary by jurisdiction; do not describe AIRTRIP as creating a universal product indication. Continue a defined response and taper plan instead of stopping all follow-up once steroids can be withdrawn.')],['AIRTRIP','ANAKLABEL','ESC2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Rilonacept Loading, Maintenance and Safety',
 'Which regimen matches the US-labeled adult recurrent-pericarditis dosing of rilonacept?',
 '320 mg subcutaneously once as a loading dose, then 160 mg subcutaneously once weekly',[('320 mg subcutaneously once as a loading dose, then 160 mg every two weeks', 'This is an interval-reduction strategy rather than the standard adult initiation and maintenance regimen. Any off-label interval change needs an individualized specialist plan; the labeled schedule starts maintenance one week after loading and continues weekly.'), ('160 mg subcutaneously once as a loading dose, then 160 mg once weekly', 'One hundred and sixty milligrams is the adult maintenance dose. The standard adult initiation uses a total loading dose of three hundred and twenty milligrams, rather than beginning with a single maintenance-sized injection.'), ('320 mg subcutaneously once as a loading dose, then 80 mg once weekly', 'A lower absolute maintenance amount can arise from weight-based pediatric calculations. The adult recurrent-pericarditis regimen uses one hundred and sixty milligrams weekly after the adult loading dose.')],
 [S('Use the Product-Specific Schedule','**Adult rilonacept initiation is 320 mg once, followed one week later by 160 mg weekly.** Give the loading dose as two separate 160-mg subcutaneous injections on the same day.',
    'The US indication includes recurrent pericarditis and reduction in recurrence risk in adults and children aged at least twelve years. Pediatric dosing is weight based: 4.4 mg/kg loading, maximum 320 mg, then 2.2 mg/kg weekly, maximum 160 mg.',
    'Do not substitute anakinra daily dosing or interpret a loading-dose entry in a secondary table as an instruction to repeat it each day. The prescribing information and trial schedule resolve the administration interval.'),
  S('Perform Pretreatment and Follow-Up Safety Assessment','**Do not initiate rilonacept in an active or chronic infection without appropriate assessment and management.** Evaluate TB risk, review vaccination and avoid live vaccines during treatment.',
    'Follow infection symptoms, injection-site reactions and lipid changes. Assess cardiovascular risk and manage elevated lipids according to the appropriate lipid-treatment framework rather than assuming the inflammatory benefit removes that risk.',
    'Avoid combining IL-1 blockers or using another potent cytokine inhibitor without an explicit expert indication and safety plan. Review pregnancy intentions, access, storage and injection technique. Symptoms improving after the loading dose do not justify doubling the interval independently or withdrawing all surveillance. Persistent pain requires reassessment of inflammation and alternatives, not automatic dose escalation. The weekly schedule is one component of a complete recurrent-disease plan that includes steroid withdrawal, objective response assessment and later discussion of treatment duration.')],['RILOLABEL','RHAPSODY'],style='direct')

R['RHAPSODYLTE']=('RHAPSODY long-term extension and treatment suspension analysis, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1161/JAHA.123.032516')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Remission on IL-1 Treatment and Withdrawal Risk',
 'In the RHAPSODY long-term extension, what was observed after treatment suspension at the eighteen-month decision milestone?',
 'Substantial clinical recurrence despite apparent on-treatment remission',[('Biomarker rebound without clinical recurrence after withdrawal', 'Biomarker monitoring can reveal subclinical activity, but the extension reported clinical pericarditis recurrences in the small suspension group. Withdrawal risk was not limited to a laboratory change.'), ('A low recurrence burden when on-treatment imaging showed little residual enhancement', 'Minimal residual enhancement is reassuring for current control but did not reliably identify durable off-treatment remission. Recurrences occurred despite the favorable clinical and imaging state before suspension.'), ('Similar recurrence patterns after suspension and continued treatment', 'Continued treatment maintained control in the extension, whereas six of eight patients in the suspension group experienced recurrence. These were extension treatment decisions, so the contrast is informative but is not a new large randomized duration comparison.')],
 [S('Separate Suppression From Lasting Disease Resolution','**An effective anti-inflammatory treatment can suppress the signs used to assess activity.** Their absence while receiving treatment is therefore not a guarantee of what will happen after withdrawal.',
    'In the RHAPSODY long-term extension, treatment suspension at the eighteen-month decision milestone was followed by recurrence in six of eight patients in that small suspension group.',
    'This was an extension treatment-decision observation, not a large new randomized comparison of all possible stopping schedules. It supports caution without establishing that every patient must receive lifelong treatment.'),
  S('Make a Defined, Reviewable Duration Decision','**The optimal duration and withdrawal strategy remain individualized.** Consider previous recurrence burden, steroid dependence, tolerance, infection risk, imaging, patient preference and the consequences of another flare.',
    'If withdrawing, specify follow-up, symptom reporting and the plan to confirm and treat a suspected recurrence. Distinguish new inflammatory disease from another chest-pain mechanism before restarting or escalating therapy.',
    'If continuing, periodically revisit benefit and harm rather than letting an effective prescription become indefinite by default. Colchicine continuation and other treatment changes should be discussed in the complete regimen context. A reassuring scan can contribute to the decision, but minimal residual enhancement or normal CRP cannot independently prove that the disease is cured. Explain the limits of the small withdrawal group and avoid presenting its percentage as the exact personal probability for every patient. Shared decision-making should include the uncertainties and the practical plan for the next clinical decision.')],['RHAPSODYLTE','ACC2025','ESC2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Echocardiographic Effusion Size and Hemodynamic Significance',
 'Using the ESC size convention, a circumferential effusion has a largest relevant end-diastolic echo-free separation of 16 mm. Which size category applies?',
 'Moderate',[('Small', 'The ESC small category is below ten millimeters. Sixteen millimeters is in the moderate range; preserved EF or blood pressure cannot change the anatomical size category.'), ('Large', 'The ESC large category is above twenty millimeters. Sixteen millimeters is moderate; the ten-millimeter boundary separates small from moderate, not moderate from large.'), ('Very large', 'Some other imaging schemes subdivide large collections and use a very-large category above approximately twenty-five millimeters. That subdivision does not describe a sixteen-millimeter effusion in the named ESC scheme.')],
 [S('Measure the Effusion With a Defined Method','**Describe both size and distribution.** For the ESC convention, assess the largest relevant echo-free separation at end diastole in an appropriate view rather than adding unrelated measurements.',
    'Small effusions are <10 mm, moderate effusions are 10–20 mm and large effusions are >20 mm. Other documents subdivide large and very large collections, so state the named scheme when boundaries are tested.',
    'Use several views to recognize loculation, clot, adjacent pleural fluid and epicardial fat. Serial comparisons should use comparable views and timing; a change in image plane can imitate a change in volume.'),
  S('Assess Pressure Effects Independently','**Rate of accumulation and pericardial compliance can matter more than total volume.** A rapidly developing small hemopericardium may impair filling more than a slowly accumulating large effusion.',
    'Integrate symptoms, perfusion, pulse and venous findings with chamber collapse, respiratory Doppler changes and IVC assessment. No isolated size threshold replaces the clinical hemodynamic assessment.',
    'The underlying cause also guides action: suspected purulent or malignant disease can require sampling or drainage without overt tamponade, whereas a stable noninflammatory idiopathic collection may be observed. Document the presence or absence of a concerning physiological pattern instead of reporting only a millimeter measurement. A new fall in pressure or worsening dyspnea warrants reassessment even if the previous scan called the effusion moderate. Conversely, normal EF is compatible with tamponade because the primary problem is filling rather than contractility.')],['ESC2025', 'ASE2013', 'IMAGING2024', 'ACC2025'],
 table=(['ESC echo size','Largest relevant end-diastolic separation'],[['Small','<10 mm'],['Moderate','10–20 mm'],['Large','>20 mm'],['Tamponade','A physiological diagnosis; no single size establishes it']]))

R['THYROID2014']=('ATA hypothyroidism treatment guideline, 2014','https://doi.org/10.1089/thy.2014.0028')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'A Noninflammatory Effusion in Severe Hypothyroidism',
 'An alert 38-year-old with severe primary hypothyroidism has a stable 24-mm pericardial effusion. There is no chest pain, CRP elevation, chamber collapse or respiratory Doppler evidence of impaired filling. Evaluation finds no competing malignant or infectious cause. Which initial plan is best?',
 'Levothyroxine with clinical and echocardiographic surveillance',[('Levothyroxine with high-dose ibuprofen and colchicine', 'Cause-directed thyroid replacement is appropriate, but aspirin/NSAID and colchicine treatment requires an inflammatory indication. The supplied pain-free CRP-negative collection does not establish accompanying inflammatory pericarditis.'), ('Levothyroxine with prednisone', 'Glucocorticoids can be needed for a separate immune-mediated or adrenal indication. The supplied stable hypothyroid effusion does not establish such an indication or an inflammatory process that requires prednisone.'), ('Levothyroxine with immediate pericardial drainage', 'Drainage is required for tamponade and can be appropriate for important symptoms or diagnostic concern. The supplied stable adequately investigated collection has none of those indications despite being anatomically large.')],
 [S('Distinguish Fluid Accumulation From Active Pericarditis','**Pericardial effusion is not synonymous with inflammatory pericarditis.** Hypothyroidism can produce gradual fluid accumulation through altered protein handling and lymphatic drainage.',
    'A slow course permits pericardial adaptation, so a sizable collection may occur without classic tamponade. Relative bradycardia can be a clue, but neither bradycardia nor a stable blood pressure independently excludes pressure effects.',
    'Confirm the thyroid biochemical pattern and assess the effusion’s physiology. Continue to investigate if fever, constitutional symptoms, hemorrhagic features or another clue makes an additional cause plausible.'),
  S('Treat the Cause and Follow the Response','**Levothyroxine is the cause-directed treatment in the supplied stable presentation.** Fluid resolution can take time and should be followed alongside thyroid recovery rather than judged after a few doses.',
    'A usual full-replacement estimate for an otherwise healthy adult is about 1.6 micrograms/kg/day orally, individualized to body size and clinical context. Known coronary disease calls for a lower starting dose, commonly 12.5–25 micrograms/day, with gradual titration.',
    'Reassess thyroid tests after an appropriate equilibration interval, commonly four to six weeks after a dose change, and adjust according to the relevant thyroid framework. Echo follow-up depends on size, symptoms and trajectory. New hypotension, progressive breathlessness or evidence of impaired filling requires urgent reassessment and possible drainage. Do not transfer this stable outpatient-style thyroid plan to myxedema coma or clinical tamponade, where immediate multisystem and hemodynamic care is required. The correct principle is to identify and treat the noninflammatory cause while maintaining a clear safety and surveillance plan.')],['ESC2025','THYROID2014'])

R['EFFUSION2019']=('Prospective outcomes of idiopathic chronic large pericardial effusions, 2019','https://doi.org/10.1136/heartjnl-2018-313532')
R['EFFUSION2020']=('Long-term outcome of drainage in chronic large CRP-negative idiopathic effusions, 2020','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32345471/')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'A Chronic Large Idiopathic Effusion Without Inflammation',
 'An asymptomatic patient has a 28-mm circumferential effusion unchanged for six months. A thorough evaluation is unrevealing, CRP is repeatedly normal, and there are no pressure effects or concerning CMR inflammatory findings. Which plan best reflects the revised contemporary approach?',
 'Structured surveillance with clinical review and serial echocardiography',[('Elective pericardiocentesis with prolonged catheter drainage', 'Older approaches emphasized preventive drainage of chronic large collections. More recent evidence supports observation for carefully evaluated asymptomatic noninflammatory idiopathic cases; persistence and size alone do not establish an intervention requirement.'), ('A preventive surgical pericardial window', 'A window can be useful for recurrent symptomatic effusion, selected malignant disease or failed percutaneous management. Its procedural risks are not justified automatically by this stable asymptomatic collection.'), ('An empirical course of prednisone and colchicine', 'Anti-inflammatory treatment requires an inflammatory indication. The supplied repeatedly CRP-negative collection without pericarditis evidence does not establish that the drugs will reduce the fluid.')],
 [S('Apply Observation Only After the Relevant Evaluation','**This conservative approach applies to an adequately investigated, clinically stable, noninflammatory phenotype.** It does not apply to suspected bacterial disease, an unresolved malignant concern or impaired filling.',
    'Record size, duration, distribution, symptoms and hemodynamic findings. Reassess thyroid, renal, immune, infectious and malignant possibilities according to the history and course rather than assuming every long-standing effusion is idiopathic.',
    'A normal CRP is one component of the phenotype; persistent symptoms or tissue inflammation can change the interpretation. The supplied patient has repeated reassuring clinical and objective assessments.'),
  S('Understand Why the Older Drainage Rule Changed','**A prospective cohort of one hundred patients reported a tamponade risk of about 2.2% per year, with better recurrence/complication-free outcomes in conservatively managed patients.** This observational evidence informed the newer risk-benefit balance.',
    'The finding is not a randomized proof that drainage is harmful in every patient. Selection and underlying cause matter, and a symptomatic or hemodynamically important effusion remains a different problem.',
    'Arrange a defined review interval, often roughly every three to six months for a stable moderate-to-large collection, adapted to trajectory and access to care. Explain urgent reassessment for new breathlessness, presyncope, hypotension or chest discomfort. Repeat etiological evaluation if the size increases or the phenotype changes. Drainage can be appropriate for evolving symptoms, pressure effects or a management-changing diagnostic question; it is not required solely to make the echo measurement normal. Observation is active follow-up with clear escalation criteria.')],['EFFUSION2019','EFFUSION2020','ESC2025'])

R['TBDIAG2014']=('Prospective comparison of pericardial Xpert MTB/RIF, ADA and unstimulated interferon-gamma, 2014','https://doi.org/10.1186/1741-7015-12-101')
R['TBDIAG2017']=('ATS/IDSA/CDC tuberculosis diagnostic guideline, 2017','https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciw694')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Investigating Suspected Tuberculous Pericarditis',
 'In a high-TB-burden setting, a patient with constitutional symptoms has a lymphocyte-predominant pericardial effusion and fluid ADA of 62 U/L. Initial pericardial-fluid Xpert MTB/RIF is negative. Which diagnostic interpretation is best?',
 'TB remains plausible; pursue culture and further targeted confirmation',[
 ('The negative fluid Xpert excludes active pericardial TB','Pericardial-fluid molecular testing has limited sensitivity in paucibacillary disease. A negative result cannot independently exclude a clinically convincing extrapulmonary presentation.'),
 ('The ADA result establishes microbiologically definite TB','An elevated ADA supports a probable diagnosis in the relevant context, but is not itself demonstration of the organism. Other inflammatory causes and local assay performance must be considered.'),
 ('A positive blood IGRA would establish the effusion as tuberculous','IGRA indicates immune sensitization to TB antigens and does not localize active disease to the pericardium. It cannot replace culture, molecular or appropriately interpreted tissue evidence.')],
 [S('Distinguish Definite From Supportive Evidence','**Definite TB pericarditis requires appropriate demonstration of TB in pericardial fluid or tissue, by microbiological or relevant molecular methods, or diagnostic tissue evidence.** A supportive biomarker must not be mislabeled as culture confirmation.',
    'Fluid ADA is often interpreted around a 40-U/L threshold in the appropriate lymphocytic clinical setting, but cut-offs and specificity vary. Unstimulated pericardial interferon-gamma can be diagnostically useful where available and is different from a blood IGRA.',
    'Send adequate specimens for mycobacterial culture and drug-resistance assessment as well as the rapid test. A negative smear or Xpert does not make those complementary investigations redundant.'),
  S('Use the Whole Disease Distribution','**Probable TB can be supported by compatible pericardial disease, TB proven elsewhere and contextual fluid findings.** Search for accessible pulmonary, nodal or other extracardiac sites that can improve diagnostic yield.',
    'Pericardial biopsy may be appropriate when the diagnosis remains unresolved or tissue is needed to distinguish TB, malignancy and other causes. Granulomas require interpretation with microbiology, epidemiology and alternative diagnoses.',
    'Assess HIV status and evaluate for disseminated disease. A clinically unstable effusion needs hemodynamic treatment while diagnostic work proceeds; microbiological completion is not a prerequisite to relieving tamponade. Conversely, a stable patient with weak supporting evidence should not receive an uncritical lifelong TB label from one biomarker. Treatment decisions in a strongly suspected case balance diagnostic probability, the danger of delay, local resistance patterns and the opportunity to obtain useful specimens before therapy.')],['TBDIAG2014','TBDIAG2017','ESC2025'],
 table=(['Finding','Diagnostic role'],[['Positive pericardial culture or appropriate molecular test','Direct etiological evidence; pursue susceptibility information'],['Lymphocytic fluid with elevated ADA','Supportive, context-dependent; not microbiological confirmation'],['Unstimulated pericardial interferon-gamma','Useful local biomarker where validated'],['Blood IGRA','Sensitization evidence; does not establish the site or activity']]))

R['IMPI']=('IMPI randomized adjunctive prednisolone trial in tuberculous pericarditis, 2014','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1407380')
R['WHOTB']=('WHO tuberculosis treatment guidance: standard adult first-line drug doses','https://iris.who.int/bitstream/handle/10665/44165/9789241547833_eng.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Tuberculous Pericarditis, HIV and Adjunctive Steroids',
 'An adult with HIV has confirmed drug-susceptible tuberculous pericarditis, without TB meningitis or another reason for prolonged treatment. Which plan best fits the ESC 2025 pericardial framework?',
 'Standard six-month multidrug TB treatment without routine adjunctive prednisolone',[('Standard six-month multidrug TB treatment plus a six-week prednisolone course', 'Adjunctive steroids can reduce some inflammatory complications, but IMPI did not improve the primary composite and identified an HIV-associated malignancy concern. ESC guidance therefore advises against routine adjunctive use in HIV-infected individuals.'), ('Use two months of four drugs followed by ten months of isoniazid and rifampicin for uncomplicated pericardial disease', 'Longer courses can be indicated for selected TB sites, resistance or complicated circumstances. Pericardial TB alone does not generally require extending an otherwise appropriate drug-susceptible regimen to twelve months.'), ('Use the four-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin pulmonary-TB regimen as the default pericardial regimen', 'Shorter regimens have defined pulmonary-TB eligibility and supporting trials. Their results should not be automatically extrapolated to this extrapulmonary pericardial presentation.')],
 [S('Treat the Infection With a Complete Regimen','**A standard drug-susceptible pericardial-TB course is two months of isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol, then four months of isoniazid and rifampicin.** Adjust to susceptibility, organ function and the applicable TB programme.',
    'Conventional daily adult target doses include isoniazid 5 mg/kg, usually maximum 300 mg; rifampicin 10 mg/kg, usually maximum 600 mg; pyrazinamide 25 mg/kg; and ethambutol 15 mg/kg. Weight-band formulations and local protocols determine the actual dispensed doses.',
    'Monitor hepatic toxicity, ethambutol visual effects, renal dosing and adherence. Provide pyridoxine for appropriate neuropathy-risk patients, including those with HIV, and coordinate rifamycin interactions with antiretroviral treatment.'),
  S('Interpret IMPI Without Turning Steroids Into a Universal Rule','**IMPI did not significantly reduce its primary composite of death, tamponade requiring drainage or constriction with adjunctive prednisolone.** It showed reductions in selected secondary outcomes but increased malignancy, particularly HIV-associated cancer.',
    'ESC 2025 considers adjunctive corticosteroids reasonable in selected patients without HIV and advises avoiding them routinely in HIV-infected individuals. This is separate from a distinct indication such as a specifically diagnosed immune-reconstitution complication.',
    'HIV care, infection staging and antiretroviral coordination are essential, but do not substitute for effective TB therapy. Drain an effusion when hemodynamic or diagnostic indications require it and follow for constrictive physiology. A fall in CRP is not proof of sterilization, and an inflammatory complication does not justify shortening the antimicrobial course. Reassess susceptibility, adherence and alternative pathology if the course deteriorates despite treatment. ESC 2025 recommends pericardiectomy assessment if tuberculous pericardial disease is not improving or is deteriorating after four to eight weeks of appropriate TB therapy; do not defer a worsening TB-associated constrictive course solely to complete a generic three-to-six-month noninfectious trial.')],['IMPI','WHOTB','ESC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Source Control in Purulent Pericarditis',
 'A septic patient has culture-positive purulent pericarditis. Despite appropriate IV antibiotics and an image-guided catheter, thick loculated material remains poorly drained and compresses the heart. Which source-control strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Urgent surgical drainage with loculation disruption and directed IV antibiotics',[('Continue catheter drainage with image-guided repositioning', 'Image-guided catheter drainage can succeed for a suitable fluid collection. Persistent thick loculated pus causing compression despite inadequate drainage calls for a strategy that can clear the loculations completely, as available with surgical drainage.'), ('Use serial image-guided needle aspirations', 'Aspiration can provide an initial diagnostic or emergency intervention. Repeated limited aspirations do not reliably clear thick infected loculations and permit ongoing infection and organization.'), ('Perform balloon pericardiotomy as the preferred next procedure', 'Balloon pericardiotomy has selected roles in recurrent effusions, particularly malignant disease. It is not the preferred way to clear persistent thick infected material requiring direct complete drainage.')],
 [S('Treat a Pericardial Space Infection as a Source-Control Emergency','**Purulent pericarditis requires prompt antimicrobial treatment and adequate drainage.** It is different from treating presumed idiopathic inflammatory pain with an NSAID and colchicine.',
    'Obtain pericardial and blood cultures and assess associated pulmonary, mediastinal, postoperative or bloodstream infection. Include bacterial, anaerobic and fungal considerations according to exposure and immune status.',
    'Begin empirical IV coverage for likely organisms and local resistance patterns while specimens are processed, then narrow to the pathogen and susceptibility. ESC 2025 advises an individualized course until fever and clinical signs resolve, with a minimum duration of three weeks; associated bloodstream or deep infection can require longer treatment.'),
  S('Choose the Procedure That Can Clear the Actual Collection','**Surgical drainage is indicated when infected material cannot be adequately removed percutaneously.** Direct access permits breaking loculations, removing thick material and maintaining drainage.',
    'Selected intrapericardial fibrinolytic strategies can be considered by experienced teams, but do not use them to postpone necessary surgery when compression or uncontrolled sepsis persists. The supplied failed catheter and thick loculations make complete source control the priority.',
    'Monitor perfusion, organ function, cultures and serial imaging rather than equating a smaller amount of external catheter output with improvement. Residual organization can progress to constrictive disease, so follow-up remains necessary after sepsis resolves. A drainage specimen is also an opportunity to confirm microbiology and identify an unexpected cause; it is not a reason to wait for final culture before stabilizing an infected compressive collection. Steroids or other immune-directed treatment cannot replace antimicrobial therapy and drainage in this setting.')],['ESC2025','ACC2025'])

R['KIDNEYPERI']=('ASN clinical approach to kidney failure and pericarditis, 2023','https://doi.org/10.2215/CJN.07470622')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Pericarditis After Missed Dialysis',
 'A patient on maintenance hemodialysis misses several sessions and develops a pericardial rub and a 14-mm effusion. There is no tamponade or evidence of infection, malignancy or another specific cause. Which initial strategy best addresses the likely mechanism?',
 'Intensify hemodialysis with careful ultrafiltration and serial echocardiographic assessment',[('Prednisone with the usual maintenance dialysis schedule', 'Steroids can be appropriate for a selected immune or refractory indication. The clear period of underdialysis makes correction of delivered dialysis the initial cause-directed step, rather than adding prednisone without addressing the trigger.'), ('Ibuprofen and colchicine with the usual maintenance dialysis schedule', 'These drugs treat selected nonuremic inflammatory disease, but do not correct inadequate dialysis. Kidney failure also increases bleeding, residual-function and colchicine-toxicity concerns.'), ('Pericardiocentesis followed by the usual maintenance dialysis schedule', 'Drainage is urgent for tamponade and can be needed for enlarging, persistent or important symptomatic effusions. The supplied stable moderate effusion after missed sessions permits initial monitored dialysis intensification.')],
 [S('Identify Inadequate Clearance and Reassess Other Causes','**Pericarditis in kidney failure is not automatically uremic.** Review infection, malignancy, immune disease, medications, infarction and procedure-related complications as appropriate.',
    'The conventional timing distinction places uremic pericarditis before or within roughly eight weeks of dialysis initiation and dialysis-associated disease after that period. Missed or inadequate treatment can create a uremic contribution even in an established dialysis patient.',
    'Assess delivered dialysis, access function, recent interruptions and the clinical trajectory. The usual diffuse ECG pattern may be absent; a normal ECG does not overrule a rub and compatible course.'),
  S('Intensify Treatment Without Destabilizing Filling','**Frequent dialysis with modest ultrafiltration and serial assessment can treat the cause while avoiding abrupt preload loss.** A published ASN clinical approach uses approximately four-hour sessions daily or six times weekly for seven to fourteen days, individualized to the patient.',
    'Heparin-free dialysis or another appropriate anticoagulation strategy can be considered because hemorrhagic complications are a concern. Use lower ultrafiltration rates when increased pericardial pressure makes filling vulnerable.',
    'Monitor symptoms and repeat focused echo frequently during the initial course; the published approach reassesses about every three days. New pressure effects require drainage rather than continuing a conservative plan through deterioration. An effusion that persists or enlarges despite adequate intensified dialysis also warrants reassessment and possible intervention. Full-dose NSAIDs, steroids and colchicine should not be added reflexively in kidney failure, where benefits and toxicities differ from uncomplicated idiopathic pericarditis. The goal is adequate clearance with maintained perfusion, not simply rapid removal of the largest possible fluid volume.')],['KIDNEYPERI','ESC2025'])

R['ONCOESC2022']=('ESC cardio-oncology guideline, 2022: pericardial disease','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehac244')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Recurrent Malignant Pericardial Effusion',
 'A patient with lung cancer and preserved functional status has recurrent symptomatic cytology-proven malignant effusion after two adequate catheter-drainage episodes. The recurrent pocket has no safe percutaneous access on expert imaging. Expected survival is several months or longer, and surgery is feasible. Which strategy offers appropriate durable recurrence control?',
 'A pericardial window integrated with the oncological treatment plan',[('Further image-guided needle aspiration when symptoms recur', 'Aspiration can relieve an accessible collection or suit selected palliative goals. This repeatedly recurrent pocket lacks a safe percutaneous route, so a durable surgical strategy is better matched to the anatomy and clinical course.'), ('Radical pericardiectomy', 'Pericardiectomy is used principally for important constrictive disease and selected refractory situations. Its greater operative burden is not the routine first surgical solution for recurrent malignant fluid when a drainage window can address the problem.'), ('Repeat catheter drainage with intrapericardial sclerosis', 'Catheter-based prolonged drainage and selected intrapericardial therapies can reduce malignant-effusion recurrence when access is suitable. The supplied unsafe percutaneous route prevents treating that as the preferred practical strategy here.')],
 [S('Confirm the Cause Rather Than Inferring It From Cancer History','**An effusion in a patient with cancer is not necessarily malignant.** Infection, radiation, treatment-related inflammation, renal disease and other causes remain possible.',
    'Cytology with an appropriate cell block is important when fluid is sampled; selected tissue investigation can improve an unresolved assessment. A negative cytology result does not exclude every malignant effusion.',
    'Assess hemodynamic urgency, recurrence pattern, loculation, performance status, prognosis and patient goals. Clinical tamponade requires relief regardless of the later recurrence-control choice.'),
  S('Match Recurrence Control to Benefit and Burden','**A surgical window can provide sustained drainage after recurrent symptomatic collections.** It is one option alongside prolonged catheter drainage, selected balloon procedures and disease-specific oncological strategies.',
    'The appropriate procedure depends on anatomy, local expertise, prior response and goals. A short expected survival or major procedural burden can favor a less invasive palliative plan rather than the same operation for every patient.',
    'Systemic cancer treatment may control the underlying process but cannot be relied on to relieve an acutely compressive collection immediately. Selected intrapericardial therapies are used in specific cancers and centers, with a distinct risk-benefit assessment. Continue to monitor for reaccumulation, drain complications and constrictive consequences. Do not interpret a technically successful window as control of the malignancy itself. Explain the practical aim—fewer recurrent symptomatic collections—and distinguish that from an unsupported survival claim or a need to remove the entire pericardium.')],['ONCOESC2022','ESC2025','ACC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Interpretation of Pericardial Fluid Biochemistry',
 'A drained pericardial sample meets a pleural Light exudate threshold. Which interpretation best follows the international pericardial-imaging position statement?',
 'Do not use pleural Light thresholds alone to assign the pericardial cause or an inflammatory diagnosis',[
 ('Classify the pericardial fluid as inflammatory whenever any one pleural threshold is met','The any-one rule belongs to the validated pleural framework. Normal pericardial fluid can satisfy those biochemical thresholds, so direct transfer can overclassify inflammation.'),
 ('Require all three pleural thresholds before diagnosing an inflammatory pericardial exudate','Changing the rule from one threshold to three does not create a validated pericardial classification. The problem is the biological and validation context, not simply insufficient strictness.'),
 ('Use the pleural serum-to-fluid albumin gradient as the definitive pericardial replacement','Albumin gradients help interpret selected pleural pseudoexudates. They are not a universally validated stand-alone replacement for pericardial etiological evaluation.')],
 [S('Recognize the Different Fluid Compartment','**Pericardial fluid composition cannot be assumed to match pleural-fluid physiology.** Protein and LDH can be relatively high in normal pericardial fluid, creating a misleading exudate label.',
    'For pleural effusions, Light criteria use a fluid-to-serum protein ratio >0.5, fluid-to-serum LDH ratio >0.6, or fluid LDH above two-thirds of the serum laboratory upper normal limit. These are pleural criteria, not validated proof of pericardial inflammation.',
    'A numerical exudate designation also does not identify the organism, establish cancer or determine whether the patient has tamponade. Those are separate etiological and physiological questions.'),
  S('Select Tests That Address a Plausible Cause','**Interpret sampled fluid together with the clinical phenotype, imaging and specific laboratory studies.** Cell count and differential, cytology and relevant microbiology answer complementary questions.',
    'Send bacterial cultures for suspected infection and mycobacterial testing when TB is plausible. Cytology, selected flow cytometry and tissue assessment can clarify malignant or hematological causes; sample quality and yield matter.',
    'For an unusual appearance, consider cause-directed tests such as hematocrit for hemorrhagic material or triglyceride/chylomicron assessment for suspected chyle. A lymphocyte-predominant sample is not specific for TB, and a negative single test does not exclude all causes. Use inflammatory markers and tissue findings to assess an inflammatory syndrome, and assess filling physiology independently. The reason to avoid uncritical Light classification is to prevent a laboratory label from replacing an actual diagnosis or triggering unnecessary anti-inflammatory therapy in a noninflammatory collection.')],['IMAGING2024','ACC2025'],style='direct')

R['NTEP2025']=('India NTEP national guidance, 2025: drug-susceptible treatment pathway','https://tbcindia.mohfw.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/National-Guidelines-for-Management-of-DR-TB_27-3-2025.pdf')
# Preserve the explicitly named ESC regimen while teaching the Indian programme distinction.
for r in rows:
    if r['title']=='Tuberculous Pericarditis, HIV and Adjunctive Steroids':
        h,bs=r['sections'][0];text,kids=bs[0]
        kids.append('For Indian programme questions, NTEP uses 2HRZE/4HRE, retaining ethambutol in continuation. Distinguish the named national regimen from the ESC/WHO-style 2HRZE/4HR description rather than silently dropping the programme-specific drug.')
        r['refs'].append('NTEP2025')

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Pulsus Paradoxus and Its Limitations',
 'Which finding defines pulsus paradoxus during spontaneous breathing?',
 'An inspiratory fall in systolic arterial pressure greater than 10 mmHg',[
 ('An inspiratory rise in jugular venous pressure','This describes Kussmaul physiology, a failure of right-sided filling accommodation. It is associated with constriction, restrictive myocardial disease and selected RV conditions rather than defining an arterial systolic pulse variation.'),
 ('An expiratory fall in systolic arterial pressure greater than 10 mmHg','The conventional pulsus-paradoxus definition concerns inspiration during spontaneous breathing. Positive-pressure ventilation can alter the direction of respiratory pressure effects, so that setting needs its own interpretation.'),
 ('An inspiratory fall in diastolic arterial pressure greater than 10 mmHg','The diagnostic bedside measurement is the respiratory difference in systolic pressure. Diastolic pressure variation is not the defining threshold and should not be substituted for it.')],
 [S('Understand Exaggerated Ventricular Interaction','**In tamponade, a constrained total cardiac volume exaggerates the effect of inspiration on ventricular filling.** Increased right-sided filling shifts the septum and reduces effective LV filling and stroke volume.',
    'Measure with a slowly deflating manual cuff: identify the pressure at which sounds are heard only during expiration and the pressure at which they persist throughout respiration. Their difference estimates the respiratory systolic variation.',
    'The term paradoxus refers to an exaggerated normal phenomenon, not an obligatory reversal of the pulse or a requirement for alternating strong and weak beats.'),
  S('Use the Sign Within the Clinical Context','**Pulsus paradoxus supports tamponade but is not specific to it.** Severe obstructive airway disease, tension pneumothorax and other marked cardiopulmonary interactions can produce a similar arterial pattern.',
    'Its absence does not reliably exclude tamponade, especially with regional postoperative compression, elevated LV filling pressure or other circumstances that alter the usual ventricular interaction. Interpret mechanically ventilated patients separately.',
    'Kussmaul sign, pulsus alternans and an irregular pulse answer different questions: venous filling accommodation, alternating ventricular performance and rhythm, respectively. Assess perfusion and urgent imaging when tamponade is plausible rather than waiting to demonstrate a cuff threshold in a collapsing patient. A large variation in a tachypneic patient also needs correlation with respiratory effort and rhythm. Treatment follows the actual cause; observing the sign does not justify pericardiocentesis without evidence that a pericardial collection is producing the compromise.')],['ESC2025','ASE2013'],style='direct',
 table=(['Bedside finding','Meaning'],[['Pulsus paradoxus','Inspiratory systolic pressure fall >10 mmHg during spontaneous breathing'],['Kussmaul sign','Inspiratory rise or failure of normal decline in JVP'],['Pulsus alternans','Beat-to-beat alternation in pulse amplitude with regular rhythm']]))

R['ALTERNANS']=('Primary clinical study of electrical alternans mechanism in pericardial effusion','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/647775/')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Electrical Alternans in a Large Pericardial Effusion',
 'A patient with a large circumferential effusion has regular sinus tachycardia. Every QRS is preceded by a P wave; alternate QRS complexes differ in amplitude and axis, while QRS complexes remain narrow and RR intervals remain stable. Which mechanism best explains the ECG pattern?',
 'Movement of the heart within the fluid-filled pericardial sac',[
 ('Alternating strength of ventricular contraction','Mechanical pulsus alternans is beat-to-beat variation in arterial pulse strength and can occur in severe ventricular dysfunction. It does not by itself explain the alternating surface QRS vector in this effusion-associated pattern.'),
 ('Alternating normal and premature ventricular beats','Ventricular bigeminy can create alternating QRS forms, but premature timing, different ventricular activation and coupling or compensatory-pause features help identify it. The supplied regular sinus activation and stable QRS duration do not support that explanation.'),
 ('Alternating His–Purkinje conduction block','Alternating aberrancy or bundle-branch block can change QRS morphology and usually changes its width or conduction pattern. Here the stable narrow activation with amplitude and axis changes in a large effusion favors positional alternans.')],
 [S('Relate the Electrical Vector to Cardiac Position','**A large effusion can permit pendular motion and rotation of the heart within the pericardial space.** The changing relation between myocardium and surface electrodes produces beat-to-beat variation in QRS amplitude or axis.',
    'Low QRS voltage and sinus tachycardia can accompany the pattern. Their absence does not exclude an effusion, and neither low voltage nor tachycardia alone is specific for a pericardial cause.',
    'First confirm lead stability, rhythm and the presence or absence of premature beats or changing conduction. Movement artefact and arrhythmia must not be mislabeled as a mechanical swinging-heart phenomenon.'),
  S('Distinguish the ECG Clue From the Hemodynamic Diagnosis','**Electrical alternans raises concern for a substantial effusion and possible tamponade but does not establish the pressure effect by itself.** Assess perfusion and obtain urgent echocardiographic evaluation in the relevant clinical setting.',
    'Mechanical pulsus alternans concerns alternating arterial pulse amplitude; pulsus paradoxus concerns respiratory systolic-pressure variation. These are different observations and can coexist without being interchangeable definitions.',
    'Echo should assess the collection, chamber behavior and filling physiology, with attention to regional compression or altered loading. A large collection can be tolerated if it accumulates slowly, while a smaller rapidly accumulating collection can cause shock without the classic ECG. Drain promptly when clinical tamponade is established, using the route appropriate to the cause and anatomy. Do not wait for electrical alternans before treating a convincing unstable presentation, and do not perform drainage solely because an alternating ECG pattern appears without a causative compressive collection.')],['ALTERNANS','ASE2013','ESC2025'],
 table=(['Observation','Distinguishing feature'],[['Effusion-associated electrical alternans','Alternating surface QRS vector from changing cardiac position'],['Ventricular bigeminy','Premature ventricular activation and a characteristic timing pattern'],['Alternating bundle-branch conduction','Changing ventricular conduction morphology or duration'],['Mechanical pulsus alternans','Alternating arterial pulse strength; an ECG alternans pattern is not required']]))

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Timing of Right-Sided Chamber Collapse',
 'Which pair of chamber-wall findings is conventionally associated with tamponade when intracardiac pressures are not already markedly elevated?',
 'RA inversion predominantly during ventricular systole and RV collapse in early ventricular diastole',[('RA inversion predominantly during ventricular systole and RV collapse during ventricular systole', 'The atrial component fits a usual pattern, but RV collapse is most characteristic when early diastolic RV pressure is low. Systolic RV inward motion must not be interpreted as the equivalent finding.'), ('RA inversion only during atrial contraction and RV collapse in late ventricular diastole', 'Normal atrial contraction and other motion can alter the RA contour. The relevant sustained inversion occurs when external pressure exceeds low atrial pressure, and characteristic RV collapse is early rather than late diastolic.'), ('RA inversion predominantly during ventricular filling and RV collapse synchronously with ventricular contraction', 'This pair assigns RV compression to ventricular systole rather than its characteristic early diastolic low-pressure phase. Confirm timing with simultaneous ECG and valve motion instead of treating an unspecified event after the QRS as a defined phase.')],
 [S('Relate Collapse to Transmural Pressure','**A chamber can invert when pericardial pressure exceeds its intracavitary pressure.** The timing reflects the chamber’s low-pressure phase, not simply its location next to the largest fluid pocket.',
    'RA inversion often begins near late ventricular diastole and extends into ventricular systole, corresponding to atrial relaxation. Some descriptions call this atrial diastolic collapse; specify the chamber and ventricular timing to avoid terminology confusion.',
    'RV collapse is typically early ventricular diastolic, when RV pressure is lowest. Confirm the phase using ECG, mitral or tricuspid opening and properly aligned M-mode or cine images.'),
  S('Assess Duration, Loading and the Entire Syndrome','**Sustained RA inversion is more persuasive than a brief contour change.** A duration greater than roughly one-third of the cardiac cycle, often expressed as >0.34, increases specificity in the appropriate context.',
    'Hypovolemia can permit chamber collapse at lower external pressure. Conversely, pulmonary hypertension, RV hypertrophy or elevated filling pressure can delay or prevent the usual right-sided signs.',
    'No chamber sign alone establishes the required intervention. Integrate pressure, perfusion, venous findings, respiratory Doppler and the cause or distribution of the collection. Loculated postoperative blood may compress a left-sided chamber and evade routine right-sided criteria. A misleading phase assignment can create either an unwarranted emergency diagnosis or false reassurance. The practical goal is to demonstrate that a collection is impairing filling in the supplied clinical state, rather than merely detecting motion toward the ventricular cavity.')],['ASE2013','ESC2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Respiratory Doppler Variation in Tamponade',
 'A spontaneously breathing patient with a circumferential effusion is in sinus rhythm. Which respiratory Doppler pattern most supports exaggerated ventricular interaction from tamponade?',
 'Mitral E velocity falls during inspiration while tricuspid E velocity rises substantially',[
 ('Mitral and tricuspid E velocities both rise substantially during inspiration','The usual spontaneous-breathing interaction produces opposite effects on LV and RV inflow. Parallel inspiratory increases are not the characteristic reciprocal tamponade pattern.'),
 ('Mitral E velocity rises during inspiration while tricuspid E velocity falls','This reverses the conventional spontaneous-breathing pattern. Ventilation mode and acquisition must be checked before assigning the meaning of respiratory variation.'),
 ('Mitral and tricuspid E velocities remain nearly unchanged throughout respiration','Minimal variation is not the expected supportive pattern in this specified rhythm and breathing setting. Its absence still requires contextual assessment rather than an absolute rule excluding tamponade.')],
 [S('Acquire Inflow With a Respiratory Reference','**Spontaneous inspiration increases right-sided filling and reduces LV filling within the constrained pericardial space.** Pulsed-wave inflow recordings should be interpreted alongside a respiratory signal and ECG.',
    'Common supportive thresholds are an inspiratory mitral E decrease greater than about 25%–30% and a tricuspid E variation greater than about 40%–60%, using the laboratory’s defined measurement convention.',
    'Use several respiratory cycles and comparable beats. Tachycardia, E/A fusion, ectopy and atrial fibrillation can make a single-beat percentage unreliable.'),
  S('Recognize Conditions That Alter the Pattern','**The thresholds are supportive physiological findings, not a stand-alone drainage rule.** Respiratory effort, obstructive lung disease, volume status and high filling pressure can alter their specificity or expression.',
    'Positive-pressure ventilation changes intrathoracic pressure transmission and can blunt, reverse or obscure the classic spontaneous-breathing variation. Do not apply an inspiratory rule without identifying the ventilation context.',
    'Constrictive pericarditis can also produce marked respiratory inflow variation, so the presence of variation does not by itself distinguish effusive tamponade from constriction. Examine the effusion, chamber collapse, septal motion, tissue Doppler and venous-flow findings as appropriate. Urgent management depends on the patient’s perfusion and the integrated mechanism. A patient with convincing clinical tamponade should not be denied treatment because an irregular rhythm prevents obtaining a tidy percentage, while a large variation in severe respiratory disease should not trigger puncture without a causative collection.')],['ASE2013','ESC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Inferior Vena Caval Plethora: A Supporting Sign',
 'Which echocardiographic finding is sensitive for elevated right-sided filling pressure but, by itself, relatively nonspecific for tamponade?',
 'A dilated IVC with reduced inspiratory collapse',[
 ('Prolonged RA inversion occupying more than one-third of the cardiac cycle','Sustained atrial inversion is more specific for an external pressure effect than IVC dilation alone in an appropriate clinical setting. Its timing and loading context still require confirmation.'),
 ('Early diastolic RV wall collapse next to an effusion','This reflects a period in which external pressure exceeds RV filling pressure. It is more directly related to pericardial compression than a large IVC, although hypovolemia and loading conditions can modify it.'),
 ('Reciprocal marked respiratory changes in mitral and tricuspid inflow','A coherent reciprocal inflow pattern provides physiological information about ventricular interaction. It also has limitations, but is not simply a marker of elevated venous pressure like IVC plethora.')],
 [S('Understand What the IVC Is Measuring','**A plethoric IVC primarily supports elevated RA pressure and limited respiratory pressure variation.** It does not identify why that pressure is high.',
    'In a spontaneously breathing adult, an IVC diameter above roughly 21 mm with less than 50% collapse is a commonly used high-RA-pressure pattern. Interpret measurement site and breathing effort correctly.',
    'RV failure, severe tricuspid regurgitation, pulmonary hypertension, volume overload and positive-pressure ventilation can produce a similar appearance without tamponade.'),
  S('Avoid Both Overdiagnosis and False Reassurance','**An abnormal IVC must be integrated with a causative collection and evidence of impaired filling.** A high venous pressure alone is not an indication for pericardiocentesis.',
    'Low-pressure tamponade is an important exception to the usual plethoric pattern: reduced circulating volume can permit hemodynamic compromise at relatively low absolute venous and pericardial pressures.',
    'Assess serial perfusion, pulse, chamber behavior and Doppler findings rather than choosing between drainage and diuresis from one subcostal image. A patient with right-heart disease may have a chronically large IVC before a new effusion occurs, so compare baseline information when available. Conversely, a small collapsible IVC in a hypovolemic patient should not end the evaluation if a collection and other findings suggest external filling constraint. The useful clinical question is whether the effusion explains the current hemodynamic change, not whether one venous diameter crosses a threshold.')],['ASE2013','IMAGING2024'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Tamponade Assessment in Severe Pulmonary Hypertension',
 'A patient with severe pulmonary arterial hypertension develops a substantial effusion and new hypoperfusion. The RV is hypertrophied, RA pressure is high, and usual RA/RV collapse is absent. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'High right-sided intracavitary pressures can mask the usual right-sided collapse signs',[
 ('High right-sided pressures make early RV collapse a more sensitive marker','Higher internal pressure opposes external compression, so a greater pericardial pressure may be required before collapse occurs. The absence of the sign therefore becomes less reassuring, not more.'),
 ('Left-sided chamber compression would favor a primary myocardial disorder','Left atrial or LV compression can occur with severe pulmonary hypertension or a regional collection. It can support an atypical pericardial pressure effect rather than establishing a primary myocardial cause.'),
 ('Preserved LV EF makes the effusion an unlikely cause of low output','Tamponade impairs filling and can reduce stroke volume despite preserved global contractility. EF cannot independently exclude a pericardial contribution to hypoperfusion.')],
 [S('Interpret Findings Against Baseline Right-Heart Physiology','**The usual right-sided collapse signs depend on low enough intracavitary pressure.** Severe pulmonary hypertension, RV hypertrophy and raised RA pressure change that prerequisite.',
    'Review the change in symptoms and perfusion, effusion distribution, left-sided compression and other imaging or hemodynamic evidence. Baseline venous distension or a large IVC may not identify the new mechanism.',
    'The differential includes worsening RV failure, arrhythmia, infection, pulmonary embolism and other causes of low output. Do not attribute every deteriorating patient with an effusion to tamponade, but do not exclude it from absent RA/RV collapse.'),
  S('Plan Any Required Drainage With Specialist Hemodynamic Support','**Relieving a clinically important collection in severe pulmonary hypertension requires particular caution.** Abrupt decompression can permit RV expansion, alter septal geometry and destabilize an already compromised circulation.',
    'Use an experienced pulmonary-hypertension and cardiac team to define the indication, monitoring and controlled drainage strategy. A meaningful hemodynamic indication is different from prophylactically emptying an incidental stable effusion.',
    'If tamponade is causing critical compromise, the higher procedural risk does not justify ignoring a treatable filling problem. Stabilization and intervention must be individualized, with serial assessment of both ventricles and readiness to manage persistent shock. Conversely, pulmonary vasodilator escalation alone is not a substitute for relieving confirmed external compression. The clinical reasoning centers on transmural pressure and the integrated course rather than applying a normal-right-heart echo checklist unchanged.')],['ASE2013','ESC2025','IMAGING2024'])

R['LOWPRESSURE']=('Clinical and hemodynamic profile of low-pressure tamponade, 2006','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.106.634584')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Low-Pressure Tamponade After Volume Depletion',
 'A patient with a known effusion becomes hypotensive after substantial diuresis. Neck veins are not distended and the IVC is small, but repeat imaging shows sustained RA inversion, early RV collapse and exaggerated reciprocal inflow variation. A cautious fluid bolus gives only transient improvement. Which diagnosis best integrates the findings?',
 'Low-pressure cardiac tamponade',[
 ('Isolated volume depletion without a pericardial contribution','Volume depletion is a contributor, but the coherent external-compression and inflow findings suggest more than isolated hypovolemia. A transient response to fluid does not prove that the pericardial constraint is irrelevant.'),
 ('Chronic constrictive pericarditis','Constriction can cause impaired filling and ventricular interaction, but usually presents with a different venous and imaging phenotype. The supplied effusion-associated right-chamber compression emerging after volume loss better fits low-pressure tamponade.'),
 ('Acute RV infarction','RV infarction can produce low output and preload sensitivity, but requires supporting ischemic evidence. The supplied compression pattern around an established effusion points to an external filling problem rather than myocardial infarction as the unifying mechanism.')],
 [S('Distinguish Absolute Pressure From the Filling Gradient','**Tamponade depends on the relation between external pericardial and intracavitary pressures.** It can occur at relatively low absolute pressures when circulating volume and chamber filling are reduced.',
    'The classic combination of high JVP, plethoric IVC and hypotension can therefore be incomplete. Diuresis, dehydration or another volume-depleting process may uncover a previously tolerated collection.',
    'A small IVC and right-chamber collapse can also occur with hypovolemia alone, so the diagnosis requires the full clinical and imaging or hemodynamic assessment rather than one sign.'),
  S('Use Resuscitation as a Bridge, Not a Diagnostic Shortcut','**Cautious volume replacement may transiently improve filling, but an ongoing causative pericardial constraint can still require drainage.** Reassess perfusion and the response continuously.',
    'Avoid additional diuresis or vasodilation while the filling problem is being clarified. Stabilize the patient and arrange expert drainage when integrated evidence establishes clinically important tamponade.',
    'Review bleeding, sepsis, drug effects and other shock mechanisms in parallel. The absence of an elevated neck-vein examination should not veto intervention when the rest of the physiology is convincing. Likewise, a fluid response does not demonstrate that the entire problem was simple dehydration. The clinical lesson is to recognize a loading-dependent presentation and treat both the volume deficit and the residual external constraint, with the definitive choice guided by serial evidence rather than an obligatory textbook venous-pressure pattern.')],['LOWPRESSURE','ASE2013','ESC2025'])

R['POSTOPTEE']=('TEE identification of loculated postoperative pericardial hematoma, primary clinical report','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2025789/')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Regional Postoperative Tamponade With Limited TTE Views',
 'After cardiac surgery, a patient develops hypotension and low output. TTE windows are poor and show little anterior fluid, but a posterior compressive hematoma remains a concern. Bedside TEE and a cardiac surgical team are available. Which next imaging step is most appropriate?',
 'Urgent bedside transesophageal echocardiography',[('Contrast-enhanced cardiac CT', 'CT can define postoperative collections and detailed anatomy when the patient is stable enough for transport. Here ongoing hypotension and poor TTE windows favor an available bedside TEE assessment before moving the patient.'), ('Contrast-enhanced CMR', 'CMR can characterize pericardial disease and myocardial tissue in selected stable patients. The unstable postoperative patient needs a rapid bedside assessment rather than a longer acquisition and transport sequence.'), ('Repeat TTE using an ultrasound-enhancing agent', 'An enhancing agent can improve ventricular blood-pool and endocardial-border assessment. It does not reliably overcome the acoustic limitations hiding a posterior pericardial clot, so TEE better answers the unresolved compressive-collection question.')],
 [S('Recognize Why Postoperative Tamponade Can Be Atypical','**Loculated blood or clot can compress a single chamber without a large circumferential effusion.** The regional anatomy can obscure usual pulsus, venous and right-chamber-collapse findings.',
    'Posterior collections may affect the left atrium or other structures poorly seen on routine anterior transthoracic views. A normal cardiac silhouette and small anterior echo-free space do not exclude this mechanism.',
    'Differentiate bleeding, vasoplegia, ischemia, valve dysfunction, RV failure and other postoperative causes while investigating the suspected collection. Several mechanisms can coexist.'),
  S('Escalate Imaging to a Technique That Answers the Question','**TEE can visualize posterior collections and their chamber effects at the bedside when TTE is inadequate.** It can guide urgent surgical decision-making without relying on the appearance of free-flowing anterior fluid.',
    'CT can be valuable when the patient is stable enough and detailed anatomical mapping is needed. It should not become a prerequisite that delays treatment of demonstrated critical compression.',
    'A thick postoperative hematoma may require surgical evacuation rather than a needle procedure designed for a free liquid pocket. Discuss the suspected location, accessibility and coagulation context with the surgical team. Continued vasoactive support can temporarily maintain pressure but does not remove a mechanical filling constraint. The correct diagnostic response is to resolve the limitation of the initial test promptly, not to interpret an inadequately visualized posterior space as an adequately excluded tamponade.')],['POSTOPTEE','ASE2013','ESC2025'])

R['PRACTICALDRAIN']=('ESC practical statement on pericardiocentesis and tamponade physiology','https://www.escardio.org/communities/councils/cardiology-practice/scientific-documents-and-publications/ejournal/volume-15/Pericardiocentesis-in-cardiac-tamponade-indications-and-practical-aspects/')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Drainage and Airway Sequencing in Critical Tamponade',
 'A spontaneously breathing patient has shock from a readily accessible free-flowing effusion, with an experienced drainage team at the bedside. Oxygenation is adequate but the patient is distressed. Which immediate sequence is best?',
 'Urgent echo-guided percutaneous drainage with local anesthesia and minimal necessary sedation',[('Urgent percutaneous drainage after induction and positive-pressure ventilation', 'Airway intervention can be unavoidable with respiratory failure or loss of airway protection. In this adequately oxygenated patient, induction and positive pressure introduce a preventable risk of circulatory collapse before the filling constraint is relieved.'), ('CT-guided percutaneous drainage after transport to the scanner', 'CT guidance can help with selected complex or poorly visualized collections in sufficiently stable patients. A readily accessible effusion causing shock can be drained by the available bedside echo-guided team without transport delay.'), ('Urgent surgical pericardial window under general anesthesia', 'Surgery is appropriate for purulence, clot, an inaccessible collection or another surgical cause. The supplied accessible free-flowing collection favors prompt percutaneous relief with less anesthetic and procedural burden.')],
 [S('Treat the Cause of Obstructive Shock','**Clinical tamponade with instability requires drainage without delay.** Use echo or other appropriate imaging guidance and an experienced operator; procedural route depends on the safe access path.',
    'Obtain monitoring, IV access and rescue support while preparing. A cautious fluid bolus may help selected underfilled patients, and vasoactive treatment can maintain perfusion temporarily, but neither is definitive relief.',
    'Do not remove preload with diuretics or unnecessary vasodilation. The patient may have preserved EF while effective ventricular filling and stroke volume are critically reduced.'),
  S('Avoid an Unnecessary Hemodynamic Insult Before Relief','**Induction-related vasodilation and positive intrathoracic pressure can worsen the filling gradient.** Preserve spontaneous breathing when feasible and minimize sedation consistent with a safe procedure.',
    'If intubation is essential, coordinate airway and drainage teams with appropriate hemodynamic preparation. The principle is not to withhold lifesaving ventilation, but to recognize that routine induction can be dangerous in untreated tamponade.',
    'The appropriate definitive route changes with the cause: purulent loculations, postoperative clot, aortic dissection or myocardial rupture may require surgery. After initial improvement, continue controlled catheter drainage, etiological testing and monitoring for reaccumulation and decompression complications. Catheter removal is commonly considered when daily output is below 30 mL and the residual collection and clinical state are reassuring, using the applicable procedural protocol. A procedural success is not the endpoint of shock assessment; persistent low output warrants evaluation for another mechanism or a drainage complication. In the supplied uncomplicated accessible liquid collection, the priority is prompt guided relief with parallel support rather than completing an avoidable diagnostic or airway sequence first.')],['ESC2025','PRACTICALDRAIN'])

R['AORTA2022']=('ACC/AHA aortic disease guideline, 2022','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001106')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Aortic Dissection With Hemopericardium',
 'A patient has confirmed acute type A aortic dissection, hemopericardium and tamponade. An emergency aortic surgical team is ready. Which definitive strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Emergency aortic repair with operative management of the tamponade',[('Create a pericardial window, followed by staged aortic repair', 'A window can manage selected fluid problems but does not control the rupturing ascending aortic source. When the aortic team is ready, definitive repair with operative tamponade relief avoids a separate preliminary drainage operation.'), ('Perform controlled small-volume pericardiocentesis before proceeding to aortic repair', 'Controlled minimal drainage is a rescue bridge when critical tamponade prevents survival to surgery and immediate surgery is unavailable. It is not a required preliminary step when definitive operative treatment is ready.'), ('Completely evacuate the hemopericardium percutaneously before aortic repair', 'Complete decompression can remove pressure limiting bleeding and abruptly increase arterial pressure. This differs from a selected minimal rescue bridge and is not the preferred sequence before available definitive repair.')],
 [S('Recognize a Surgical Cause of Tamponade','**The bleeding source is a life-threatening ascending aortic lesion.** Drainage alone treats the external constraint without controlling that source.',
    'The usual bedside needle-drainage pathway for an uncomplicated effusion cannot be transferred unchanged to aortic rupture or post-infarction free-wall rupture. Both require urgent surgical involvement.',
    'Coordinate aortic surgery, anesthesia, blood products and perfusion support. Avoid unnecessary delay once the diagnosis and surgical indication are established.'),
  S('Distinguish Definitive Treatment From Rescue Bridging','**Controlled removal of very small amounts of blood can be considered when critical tamponade prevents survival to surgery and immediate operative treatment is unavailable.** It is an expert rescue strategy, not unrestricted evacuation.',
    'ESC practical guidance describes titration to the minimum perfusing pressure, often around a systolic pressure of 90 mmHg, while expediting surgery. The aim is temporary stabilization without provoking uncontrolled bleeding.',
    'A patient who can proceed immediately to the operating room does not need a routine preliminary complete pericardiocentesis. Maintain the distinction between cautious bridging and definitive source repair when interpreting an examination option. Reassess associated aortic regurgitation, coronary involvement, neurological findings and organ malperfusion through the acute aortic pathway. An initial improvement in blood pressure does not establish that the rupture risk has ended or that the type A lesion can be managed like an uncomplicated type B dissection.')],['AORTA2022','PRACTICALDRAIN','ESC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Pericardial Decompression Syndrome',
 'After rapid removal of 1.5 L from a large chronic effusion, a patient initially improves but then develops pulmonary edema and new ventricular dysfunction. Repeat echo shows no significant reaccumulation, and urgent assessment excludes major bleeding, coronary injury and another obvious shock cause. Which complication best explains the course?',
 'Pericardial decompression syndrome',[
 ('Recurrent tamponade from immediate fluid reaccumulation','Reaccumulation can cause recurrent obstructive shock, but should be demonstrated by a causative collection and pressure effects. The supplied repeat imaging does not support it.'),
 ('Myocardial laceration with continuing hemopericardium','Needle-related injury can cause bleeding and recurrent compression. The absence of the relevant bleeding or reaccumulation findings and the new ventricular failure pattern favor a different complication.'),
 ('A procedure-related hemothorax','Pleural injury and bleeding can cause respiratory or circulatory deterioration, but require matching pleural and bleeding evidence. They do not best explain new ventricular dysfunction with pulmonary edema after relief of the effusion.')],
 [S('Recognize Paradoxical Deterioration After Initial Relief','**Pericardial decompression syndrome is hemodynamic or pulmonary deterioration after drainage, often with new LV, RV or biventricular dysfunction.** It is a diagnosis requiring exclusion of other urgent complications.',
    'Proposed mechanisms include abrupt changes in venous return, ventricular interaction, afterload and myocardial perfusion or autonomic adaptation. No single mechanism explains every reported case.',
    'Evaluate recurrent effusion, bleeding, coronary or chamber injury, pneumothorax, embolism, sepsis and other causes according to the presentation. Do not label any post-procedure hypotension as decompression syndrome without reassessment.'),
  S('Drain in a Controlled Fashion and Provide Monitored Support','**Relieve the critical filling constraint first, then continue controlled staged drainage through a catheter when appropriate.** ESC 2025 advises avoiding a large immediate evacuation when possible, commonly aiming for an initial amount below about 500 mL.',
    'This is not a rigid instruction to stop lifesaving relief at a fixed volume, and no volume guarantees prevention. Drain according to perfusion, pressure effects and procedural monitoring rather than the desire to empty the entire space immediately.',
    'Established deterioration requires intensive monitoring, repeat imaging and support tailored to the ventricular and respiratory phenotype. Manage pulmonary edema, perfusion and arrhythmia while excluding a treatable procedural cause; advanced support can be needed in severe cases. A history of a large long-standing collection should prompt particular attention to the decompression plan. Continue monitoring after initial improvement, because the important complication can emerge after the apparent procedural success rather than during needle entry.')],['ESC2025','IMAGING2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Clinical Physiology of Constrictive Pericarditis',
 'A patient has ascites, edema, elevated JVP with a brisk y descent, an inspiratory rise in JVP and an early diastolic knock. LV EF is preserved and there is little pulmonary congestion. Which diagnosis best integrates the examination?',
 'Constrictive pericarditis',[
 ('Cardiac tamponade','Tamponade can cause elevated venous pressure and low output, but usually blunts the y descent because early diastolic emptying remains constrained. A brisk y descent and knock favor rapid early filling followed by abrupt limitation.'),
 ('Restrictive cardiomyopathy','Myocardial restriction can closely mimic constriction and can produce Kussmaul sign. The knock and overall examination favor a pericardial mechanism, which still requires physiological imaging confirmation.'),
 ('Severe tricuspid regurgitation','Severe TR can produce systemic congestion, but prominent systolic venous waves, systolic hepatic-flow reversal and valve findings are more characteristic. The early diastolic knock and rapid y descent suggest a different filling limitation.')],
 [S('Recognize Rapid Early Filling Followed by Constraint','**Constriction permits brisk early diastolic filling before a noncompliant pericardial envelope limits further expansion.** This produces the clinical and hemodynamic pattern of early filling followed by abrupt interruption.',
    'A pericardial knock is an early diastolic sound caused by the abrupt limitation. Kussmaul sign reflects failure to accommodate increased venous return during inspiration; it is not unique to constriction.',
    'Ascites, edema, hepatic congestion and pleural effusions can dominate despite preserved EF. The presentation may initially be mistaken for primary liver disease or another cause of right-sided HF.'),
  S('Confirm the Mechanism Before Selecting Treatment','**Clinical clues suggest constriction but do not by themselves exclude restrictive myocardial disease or important valve pathology.** Echo should assess ventricular interdependence, tissue Doppler and venous flow.',
    'Review TB, prior cardiac surgery, mediastinal radiation, recurrent inflammatory disease and other relevant exposures. In some patients no definite cause is identified; in others pericardial and myocardial disease coexist.',
    'CT or CMR can define anatomy and CMR can assess inflammation and dynamic physiology. When noninvasive findings remain equivocal and the answer changes treatment, simultaneous invasive assessment can clarify the mechanism. Diuretics can relieve congestion but do not remove a fixed external constraint. An inflammatory potentially transient form may respond to medical treatment, while persistent significant noninflammatory constriction can require pericardiectomy. The practical examination distinction is rapid early filling with subsequent restraint, rather than assuming all venous congestion with preserved EF has the same cause.')],['ASE2013','ESC2025','IMAGING2024'])

R['MAYO2014']=('Mayo Clinic echocardiographic criteria for constrictive pericarditis, 2014','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCIMAGING.113.001613')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Integrated Echocardiographic Criteria for Constriction',
 'Which combination most strongly supports constrictive pericarditis using the original Mayo Clinic echocardiographic framework?',
 'Respiratory septal shift, medial e′ 11 cm/s and hepatic-vein expiratory diastolic reversal ratio 0.86',[('Respiratory septal shift, medial e′ 5 cm/s and hepatic-vein expiratory diastolic reversal ratio 0.30', 'Septal shift is important, but the supplied annular and venous-flow values do not provide either complementary criterion in the original combination. Reduced medial motion also prompts assessment for myocardial or mixed disease.'), ('No respiratory septal shift, medial e′ 11 cm/s and hepatic-vein expiratory diastolic reversal ratio 0.30', 'A preserved medial e-prime is supportive but cannot independently reproduce the combined respiratory-septal and complementary-criterion framework. A single favorable number should not replace integrated assessment.'), ('No respiratory septal shift, medial e′ 5 cm/s and hepatic-vein expiratory diastolic reversal ratio 0.86', 'Marked expiratory diastolic reversal is useful, but the original combined approach requires the respiratory septal component. Assess acquisition and competing venous or myocardial explanations before relying on one value.')],
 [S('Combine Interdependence With Annular or Venous Evidence','**The original Mayo approach uses respiratory ventricular septal shift plus either medial e′ ≥9 cm/s or hepatic-vein expiratory diastolic reversal ratio ≥0.79.** All three together provide a particularly persuasive coherent pattern.',
    'The primary study compared surgically established constriction with restrictive myocardial disease or severe TR. The stated combined criterion had sensitivity of 87% and specificity of 91% in that study population.',
    'The reversal ratio compares the expiratory diastolic reverse-flow velocity with the forward diastolic velocity using the appropriate acquisition. It is not simply the presence of any brief backward hepatic flow.'),
  S('Interpret the Timing and Clinical Setting','**Diastolic expiratory reversal supports constrictive physiology; systolic reversal suggests a different venous mechanism such as severe TR.** Rhythm and respiratory timing are essential to correct interpretation.',
    'Reduced medial e-prime may occur with mixed myocardial disease, prior surgery or other factors and does not universally exclude constriction. Likewise, a postoperative septal motion abnormality is not automatically respiratory ventricular interdependence.',
    'Mitral respiratory variation is useful but may be limited by AF, high filling pressure or other acquisition issues. Use CT/CMR anatomy and dynamic findings as complementary evidence. Do not equate the performance of a selected diagnostic study with universal certainty in every clinical population. If the diagnosis remains unresolved before a major intervention, obtain additional expert assessment rather than accepting or rejecting constriction from a single threshold.')],['MAYO2014','ASE2013'],style='direct',
 table=(['Original Mayo component','Supportive finding'],[['Respiratory ventricular septal shift','Required component of the stated combined approach'],['Medial mitral annular e′','≥9 cm/s'],['Hepatic-vein expiratory diastolic reversal ratio','≥0.79'],['Combined approach','Septal shift plus either of the other two components']]))

R['ANNULUS2001']=('Primary study of annulus paradoxus in constrictive pericarditis, 2001','https://doi.org/10.1161/hc3401.095705')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Annulus Reversus and Annulus Paradoxus',
 'Which pairing correctly describes the classical annular findings of constrictive pericarditis?',
 'Annulus reversus: medial e′ exceeds lateral e′; annulus paradoxus: inverse E/e′–filling-pressure relationship',[('Annulus reversus: lateral e′ exceeds medial e′; annulus paradoxus: inverse E/e′–filling-pressure relationship', 'The inverse relationship describes the classical paradox, but lateral-greater-than-medial motion is the usual normal ordering rather than annulus reversus. Lateral pericardial tethering can reverse that ordering.'), ('Annulus reversus: medial e′ exceeds lateral e′; annulus paradoxus: positive E/e′–filling-pressure relationship', 'The velocity ordering describes annulus reversus. The classical paradox instead reflects an inverse relationship observed in constriction, so the usual positive filling-pressure inference cannot simply be transferred.'), ('Annulus reversus: lateral e′ exceeds medial e′; annulus paradoxus: positive E/e′–filling-pressure relationship', 'This pairs the usual annular ordering with the usual direction of a conventional E/e-prime filling-pressure inference. Neither represents the classical constrictive reversal/paradox described in the question.')],
 [S('Explain the Reversed Annular Relationship','**Lateral annular motion can be restrained by pericardial tethering while medial longitudinal motion remains relatively preserved.** Medial e-prime can therefore exceed lateral e-prime.',
    'This differs from the common normal pattern in which lateral e-prime exceeds medial e-prime. The sign is supportive rather than obligatory and can be altered by surgery, annular pathology or mixed myocardial disease.',
    'Measure tissue velocities at the appropriate annular sites and consider rhythm and alignment. Do not compare a tissue velocity from one site with a transmitral inflow velocity from another phase and call that a reversal.'),
  S('Avoid a Misleading Filling-Pressure Estimate','**In constriction, the usual E/e-prime relationship can fail because e-prime is not reduced in the same way as in primary impaired myocardial relaxation.** The primary annulus-paradoxus study described an inverse relationship with wedge pressure.',
    'A low ratio can coexist with elevated filling pressure and clinical congestion. It does not establish that diuretics, further evaluation or a pericardial treatment pathway are unnecessary.',
    'Use septal respiratory shift, venous-flow timing, clinical findings and complementary imaging or invasive data to establish physiology. Annulus reversus and paradoxus describe different observations: spatial ordering of tissue velocities versus interpretation of the inflow-to-tissue ratio. Neither term proves an etiology such as TB or radiation. In a mixed pericardial-myocardial process, tissue velocities may be lower and the pure classical pattern less apparent, so a missing sign should lead to contextual review rather than automatic exclusion.')],['ANNULUS2001','MAYO2014','IMAGING2024'],style='direct')

R['NORMALTHICKNESS']=('Surgically proven constriction with normal pericardial thickness, 2003','https://doi.org/10.1161/01.CIR.0000087606.18453.FD')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Constrictive Physiology Despite Normal CT Thickness',
 'A patient has systemic venous congestion, respiratory septal shift, preserved medial e′ and prominent hepatic-vein expiratory diastolic reversal. CT reports no increased pericardial thickness. There is no large effusion or severe TR. Which diagnosis remains strongly supported?',
 'Constrictive pericarditis',[('Restrictive cardiomyopathy', 'Normal pericardial thickness does not establish a myocardial cause. The supplied interdependence, annular and diastolic venous pattern remains coherent with constriction rather than being overruled by static CT anatomy.'), ('Cardiac tamponade', 'Tamponade requires the relevant external pressure context, often an effusion or regional collection. The supplied absence of a causative collection and the combined constrictive physiology better support constriction.'), ('Primary RV failure', 'RV failure can cause systemic venous congestion and may alter septal geometry. It does not as well explain the combined respiratory septal shift, preserved medial annular motion and expiratory diastolic venous reversal characteristic of the supplied constrictive pattern.')],
 [S('Separate Pericardial Anatomy From Its Mechanical Effect','**A pericardium can constrain filling without being conspicuously thick on CT or pathology.** Conversely, thickening or calcification does not prove that filling is constricted.',
    'In a surgical series, eighteen percent of patients with proven constriction had no increased pericardial thickness. This observation disproves a mandatory thick-pericardium rule; it is not a prevalence estimate for every referral population.',
    'CT is excellent for calcification and anatomical mapping, but a static thickness measurement cannot replace dynamic ventricular-interaction assessment.'),
  S('Integrate the Physiological Evidence','**The diagnosis requires a coherent explanation of the congestion and filling pattern.** Review respiratory septal behavior, tissue Doppler, hepatic-flow timing and the clinical history.',
    'CMR can assess dynamic physiology, tissue inflammation and associated myocardial disease. If uncertainty remains and the answer changes a surgical decision, simultaneous invasive hemodynamics can add important information.',
    'Prior surgery or radiation can create mixed abnormalities and technical pitfalls, so use an experienced team when anatomy and physiology appear discordant. Do not recommend pericardiectomy from a calcification alone, and do not deny assessment for a potentially treatable external constraint because the pericardium is thin. Treatment selection then depends on active inflammation, reversibility, severity and operative risk rather than on thickness as a single gate. The key examination principle is that the clinically important abnormality is mechanical constraint, which may exist without the textbook anatomical appearance.')],['NORMALTHICKNESS','IMAGING2024','ESC2025'])

R['CATH2008']=('Primary invasive criteria distinguishing constriction and restrictive myocardial disease, 2008','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2007.09.039')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Invasive Assessment When Noninvasive Constriction Testing Is Equivocal',
 'A patient with prior mediastinal radiation has persistent congestion. Expert echo and CMR remain equivocal between constriction, restrictive myocardial disease and a mixed process. The result will change the treatment decision. Which investigation best resolves the physiological question?',
 'Simultaneous RV and LV pressure recordings during respiration',[('Right-heart catheterization with resting pressure assessment', 'Right-sided pressures and the RA contour characterize congestion, but overlap across constriction, myocardial restriction and valve disease. Simultaneous ventricular respiratory interaction adds the key discriminating information.'), ('CT mapping of pericardial calcium', 'CT defines calcification and anatomy and can help surgical planning. It does not directly settle the unresolved physiological relation between the ventricles or exclude a mixed myocardial process.'), ('Endomyocardial biopsy', 'Biopsy can answer a selected etiological myocardial question. It does not directly measure the pericardial-versus-myocardial filling physiology and is not the preferred replacement for the hemodynamic assessment here.')],
 [S('Record the Interaction, Not Only Static Pressures','**Constriction exaggerates ventricular interdependence and alters transmission of intrathoracic pressure to the cardiac chambers.** Simultaneous recordings preserve the respiratory relation that sequential traces can miss.',
    'During spontaneous inspiration, RV and LV systolic pressures or systolic pressure-time areas can move reciprocally in constriction. Restrictive myocardial disease more often produces concordant changes.',
    'Diastolic pressure equalization and a dip-and-plateau contour can occur in more than one disorder. They support abnormal filling but are less discriminating than a correctly acquired respiratory interaction assessment.'),
  S('Use the Result to Refine a Treatment Decision','**The test should answer whether there is a surgically relevant external constraint, a myocardial process or both.** Prior radiation makes mixed disease particularly important.',
    'The systolic area index is the inspiratory RV/LV systolic pressure-time-area ratio divided by the expiratory RV/LV area ratio. A value >1.1 supported constriction in the primary study protocol. Calculate it from simultaneous high-quality recordings rather than substituting the ratio of peak systolic pressures.',
    'Review volume status, ventilation, rhythm, catheter fidelity and respiratory effort before accepting a borderline result. A low-preload state can conceal physiology, while severe lung disease can complicate respiratory signals. Additional expert maneuvers are selected for a defined question, not performed automatically. If mixed myocardial injury predominates, removing the pericardium may not fully relieve HF. Discuss this limitation before a high-risk operation rather than assuming every equalized end-diastolic pressure has the same surgical implication.')],['CATH2008','IMAGING2024','ESC2025'])

R['EFFUSIVECONSTRICT']=('Prospective clinical and invasive study of effusive-constrictive pericarditis, 2004','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa035630')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Effusive-Constrictive Pericarditis After Adequate Drainage',
 'Before pericardiocentesis, RA pressure is 18 mmHg. After complete relief of the effusion, intrapericardial pressure is near 0 mmHg but RA pressure remains 14 mmHg with constrictive Doppler findings. Severe TR and primary RV failure have been excluded. Which diagnosis best explains the persistent congestion?',
 'Effusive-constrictive pericarditis',[
 ('Persistent tamponade from an undrained circumferential effusion','Residual compressive fluid can maintain elevated pressures, but the supplied near-zero intrapericardial pressure and adequate drainage argue against that explanation. A remaining constrictive component can persist after the fluid constraint is removed.'),
 ('Primary RV systolic failure','RV failure can leave RA pressure high after drainage and is an important alternative. The supplied functional evaluation excludes it, while the remaining respiratory filling pattern supports constriction.'),
 ('Severe tricuspid regurgitation','TR can cause persistent venous hypertension and must be assessed before attributing the pressure to visceral pericardial disease. It is excluded here and does not explain the stated constrictive physiology.')],
 [S('Recognize the Two Components','**An effusion can coexist with a constricting visceral pericardial process.** Removing fluid relieves one constraint but does not immediately normalize filling if the other remains.',
    'The classical invasive definition is failure of RA pressure to fall by at least 50% or to below 10 mmHg after intrapericardial pressure is reduced to near zero. The supplied fall from eighteen to fourteen is only about twenty-two percent and leaves the absolute pressure clearly elevated.',
    'Exclude residual loculated compression, severe TR, primary RV failure and other causes of high RA pressure. A single elevated venous pressure after a technically incomplete procedure is not sufficient.'),
  S('Reassess Inflammation and Persistence Before Surgery','**Effusive-constrictive physiology is not automatically permanent constriction.** Some cases improve with appropriate treatment of active inflammation or the underlying cause.',
    'Follow symptoms, venous pressure, echo physiology and selected CMR findings after adequate drainage. Noninvasive assessment can identify the phenotype without requiring invasive measurements in every patient.',
    'TB, malignancy, postoperative disease and idiopathic inflammatory processes can underlie the syndrome, so pursue a directed etiological evaluation. Treat the cause and assess whether an anti-inflammatory trial is justified by actual inflammatory evidence. Persistent important irreversible constriction may require expert pericardiectomy, and visceral involvement can make the operation technically demanding. Do not repeatedly drain an already decompressed space in an attempt to treat the remaining nonfluid constraint. The key decision is to recognize what persists after successful removal of the effusion and then determine its reversibility and clinical consequence.')],['EFFUSIVECONSTRICT','ESC2025','IMAGING2024'])

R['REVERSIBLE2011']=('Primary pilot study of inflammatory CMR findings and reversibility of constriction, 2011','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.111.026070')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Potentially Transient Inflammatory Constriction',
 'Four weeks after an inflammatory pericardial illness, a stable patient has constrictive physiology, raised CRP and marked pericardial edema and enhancement on CMR. There is no infection, advanced organ injury or urgent surgical instability. First-line drugs are suitable. Which initial strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Anti-inflammatory treatment with serial physiological reassessment',[('Early radical pericardiectomy', 'Surgery is appropriate for significant permanent constriction or unsuccessful medical treatment. This recent stable inflammatory phenotype provides an opportunity to test reversibility with medical treatment before undertaking an irreversible operation.'), ('Diuretic-based treatment without an anti-inflammatory regimen', 'Diuretics can reduce congestion but do not directly address active pericardial inflammation. The supplied objective inflammatory evidence supports an additional cause-directed medical trial.'), ('An IL-1 agent as the initial anti-inflammatory regimen', 'IL-1 treatment has a role in selected resistant disease or when conventional options are unsuitable. The supplied patient can receive first-line therapy and has not established that escalation requirement.')],
 [S('Identify an Inflammatory Opportunity for Reversal','**Recent onset with objective inflammatory activity supports considering transient constriction.** Pericardial edema, enhancement and elevated markers can help identify a phenotype in which medical treatment may improve physiology.',
    'A pilot CMR study associated inflammatory markers and greater enhancement with reversibility after anti-inflammatory therapy. This was predictive observational evidence, not a guarantee of recovery from one scan.',
    'Rule out a specific infection or another cause needing separate therapy before suppressing inflammation. Advanced instability or progressive organ compromise can change the urgency and suitability of a trial.'),
  S('Treat and Reassess Instead of Waiting Without a Plan','**ESC 2025 supports an anti-inflammatory trial over approximately three to six months when there is evidence of pericarditis.** Follow the patient earlier during that period and modify the plan according to response.',
    'Reference adult regimens with suitable organ function include aspirin 750–1000 mg or ibuprofen 600–800 mg every eight hours, with proton pump inhibitor gastroprotection and weight-/clearance-adjusted colchicine, commonly 0.5 mg once or twice daily. Steroids are reserved for an appropriate indication or unsuitable first-line therapy, with a deliberate taper.',
    'Track congestion, renal and hepatic function, symptoms, inflammatory markers and echo physiology; selected repeat CMR can clarify residual inflammation. Use cautious diuresis for genuine volume excess without compromising filling. A fixed calendar must not delay necessary surgery if clinical status worsens. Conversely, persistent anatomical thickening after symptom improvement does not by itself prove treatment failure. If constriction remains clinically important after an adequate trial or is clearly noninflammatory and permanent, reassess for pericardiectomy through an experienced center.')],['REVERSIBLE2011','ESC2025','IMAGING2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Pericardiectomy for Persistent Irreversible Constriction',
 'A suitable operative candidate has disabling venous congestion from confirmed constriction persisting for seven months. There is no active pericardial inflammation and no predominant myocardial explanation. Symptoms remain substantial despite careful diuresis. Which definitive strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Radical pericardiectomy',[('Pericardial window creation', 'A window drains fluid into another space and is useful for selected recurrent effusions. It does not adequately remove a diffuse fixed constricting pericardial envelope.'), ('An IL-1 inhibitor as primary disease treatment', 'IL-1 treatment addresses an appropriate inflammatory phenotype. There is no supplied active inflammation that would justify expecting it to reverse a fixed fibrotic mechanical restraint.'), ('Long-term diuretic-based management', 'Medical management can be appropriate when operative risk is prohibitive, systemic disease is advanced or myocardial pathology predominates. The supplied suitable operative candidate has disabling irreversible external constraint for which diuretics do not offer equivalent definitive relief.')],
 [S('Match Surgery to Demonstrated Permanent Constraint','**Pericardiectomy is the definitive treatment for clinically significant permanent constriction when expected benefit justifies operative risk.** Diagnosis must be established physiologically rather than inferred from calcification alone.',
    'ESC 2025 recommends pericardiectomy when there is no active inflammation or when anti-inflammatory treatment has not succeeded after an appropriate three-to-six-month period. Do not insist on a medical trial for a clearly noninflammatory fixed process simply to reach an arbitrary date.',
    'Use an experienced center because extent of resection, adhesions, calcification and visceral involvement influence both technical difficulty and completeness of relief.'),
  S('Assess What May Limit Recovery','**Mixed myocardial disease and advanced systemic consequences can reduce the benefit of removing the pericardial restraint.** Radiation-associated disease particularly warrants assessment for myocardial, valve and coronary pathology.',
    'Review renal and hepatic dysfunction, nutrition, pulmonary pressures, functional status and other surgical risks. Optimize congestion cautiously and investigate important coexisting lesions before selecting the operative plan.',
    'A limited window is not equivalent to radical removal of the constricting tissue. Conversely, an operation should not be presented as a guaranteed cure of all HF when restrictive myocardium or another major disease remains. Discuss expected symptom improvement, procedural risk and the possibility of residual congestion. For patients with prohibitive risk, individualized medical and advanced-HF care may be appropriate. The supplied combination of a confirmed fixed mechanism, substantial symptoms and suitable operative status supports definitive surgical assessment rather than indefinite anti-inflammatory escalation.')],['ESC2025','IMAGING2024','ACC2025'])

add(SECTIONS[6][0],'Post-Cardiac Injury Syndrome After Surgery',
 'Three weeks after CABG, a patient develops new pleuritic positional pain, fever without an identified infectious cause, a pleural rub, raised CRP and new pleural and pericardial effusions. Which diagnosis best unifies the delayed inflammatory presentation?',
 'Postpericardiotomy syndrome',[('Residual postoperative hemopericardium', 'Residual blood can cause a collection and pressure effects, particularly early after surgery. The delayed pleuropericardial inflammatory presentation with fever, rub and CRP elevation requires more than a simple residual collection to explain it.'), ('Decompensated heart failure', 'HF can cause pleural and pericardial fluid, but does not itself explain the new rub, pleuritic pain and febrile inflammatory pattern. Review ventricular and volume findings while assessing the post-injury syndrome.'), ('Pneumonia with parapneumonic effusion', 'Pneumonia is an important postoperative differential requiring clinical and imaging assessment. No infectious explanation has been identified, and the combined pericardial and pleural inflammatory findings favor postpericardiotomy syndrome.')],
 [S('Apply the Post-Injury Inflammatory Framework','**PCIS includes delayed inflammatory syndromes after cardiac surgery, infarction or other cardiac injury.** The course often has a latency after the inciting injury rather than representing immediate mechanical postoperative fluid alone.',
    'The proposed criteria require at least two of five findings: fever without another cause; pericardial or pleuritic pain; a pericardial or pleural rub; pericardial effusion; or pleural effusion with elevated CRP.',
    'Objective inflammation is important because effusions and nonspecific postoperative symptoms are common. Consider infection, ischemia, embolism, bleeding and HF when the presentation or course suggests them.'),
  S('Treat the Inflammatory Syndrome and Follow Complications','**Treatment follows an appropriate pericarditis anti-inflammatory approach after the relevant alternatives are assessed.** Aspirin or a suitable NSAID with colchicine and gastroprotection is usual when there is no contraindication.',
    'Aspirin is preferred when the ischemic-heart-disease context favors it; a reference adult anti-inflammatory regimen is 750–1000 mg every eight hours with gastroprotection, adjusted to tolerance and bleeding risk. Add weight-/clearance-adjusted colchicine, commonly 0.5 mg once or twice daily; an established first inflammatory episode generally needs at least three months, distinct from a one-month perioperative prevention schedule. Coordinate antithrombotic treatment, organ function and monitoring with the postoperative plan.',
    'Plan response assessment and a taper after remission, and evaluate recurrent symptoms objectively. A growing effusion, hypotension or increasing breathlessness requires renewed physiological assessment rather than assuming every later symptom is another benign inflammatory flare. Steroids and more advanced immune therapy have selected indications when first-line treatment is unsuitable or ineffective. The useful distinction is a supported delayed inflammatory syndrome involving pleura and pericardium, not simply the presence of fluid after a procedure.')],['ESC2025','ACC2025'],
 table=(['Proposed PCIS criterion','Qualification'],[['Fever','No alternative cause identified'],['Pain','Pericardial or pleuritic'],['Rub','Pericardial or pleural'],['Pericardial effusion','New relevant finding in the clinical context'],['Pleural effusion','With elevated CRP'],['Diagnostic combination','At least two criteria, with evidence supporting an inflammatory syndrome']]))

R['COPPS']=('COPPS randomized postoperative colchicine prevention trial, 2010','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehq319')
R['COPPS2']=('COPPS-2 randomized perioperative colchicine prevention trial, 2014','https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2014.11026')
add(SECTIONS[6][0],'COPPS-2: Prevention Benefit and Gastrointestinal Harm',
 'Which interpretation of COPPS-2 is correct?',
 'Perioperative colchicine reduced postpericardiotomy syndrome, with more adverse events, mainly gastrointestinal',[
 ('Colchicine reduced postoperative AF but did not reduce postpericardiotomy syndrome','The primary syndrome outcome favored colchicine. The intention-to-treat AF comparison did not reach statistical significance; the on-treatment finding must not replace the randomized primary analysis.'),
 ('Colchicine reduced postoperative pericardial and pleural effusions with no additional adverse-event burden','COPPS-2 did not show a significant reduction in that secondary fluid outcome, and adverse events were more frequent. Its findings must not be replaced by results from a different trial or substudy.'),
 ('The trial established colchicine as treatment for an already present postoperative effusion','COPPS-2 tested prevention beginning before surgery. Treatment of an established isolated postoperative collection is a distinct question with separate trial evidence.')],
 [S('Identify the Timing and Endpoint','**COPPS-2 randomized patients before cardiac surgery to weight-adjusted colchicine or placebo.** Treatment began forty-eight to seventy-two hours before surgery and continued for one month afterward.',
    'The regimen was colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for patients weighing at least 70 kg and 0.5 mg daily for those below 70 kg. This was a trial schedule, requiring the usual clinical organ-function and interaction review.',
    'Postpericardiotomy syndrome within three months occurred in 19.4% with colchicine versus 29.4% with placebo. The intention-to-treat secondary AF and pericardial/pleural-effusion comparisons were not statistically significant.'),
  S('Compare Benefits With Tolerability and the Relevant Trial','**Adverse events occurred in 20.0% with colchicine versus 11.7% with placebo, principally from gastrointestinal intolerance.** Benefit therefore does not imply unrestricted use without patient selection.',
    'The earlier COPPS trial started treatment on postoperative day three and reported syndrome reduction at twelve months, 8.9% versus 21.1%. Its timing and results should not be silently attributed to COPPS-2.',
    'Discuss prophylaxis using the applicable recommendation, surgical context, renal and hepatic status, interactions and tolerance. A preventive effect on an inflammatory syndrome does not establish a drug effect on every postoperative effusion or an independent mortality benefit. Similarly, an on-treatment AF result can be informative but is vulnerable to the consequences of discontinuation and does not override the intention-to-treat comparison. Keep prevention, treatment of established inflammatory PCIS and treatment of a noninflammatory collection as separate clinical questions.')],['COPPS2','COPPS','ESC2025'],style='direct')

R['POPE2']=('Randomized colchicine trial for established postoperative pericardial effusion, 2015','https://doi.org/10.1136/heartjnl-2015-307827')
add(SECTIONS[6][0],'An Isolated Postoperative Effusion Without Inflammation',
 'Ten days after uncomplicated cardiac surgery, an asymptomatic patient has a stable 8-mm effusion. There is no fever, rub, pleuritic pain, CRP elevation or hemodynamic impairment. Which plan is most appropriate for the effusion itself?',
 'Postoperative clinical and echocardiographic follow-up',[('High-dose aspirin and colchicine', 'These drugs treat a supported inflammatory pericardial syndrome. The supplied collection does not establish that context, and fluid alone should not be relabeled as PCIS.'), ('Prednisone-based anti-inflammatory treatment', 'Steroids can treat selected active immune-mediated or refractory pericardial disease. The supplied small noninflammatory collection has no corresponding indication, so exposure introduces harms without an established treatment target.'), ('Elective diagnostic pericardiocentesis', 'Sampling is appropriate for a meaningful diagnostic concern or drainage indication. A small stable asymptomatic collection without pressure effects does not justify an invasive procedure simply because surgery occurred recently.')],
 [S('Do Not Diagnose an Inflammatory Syndrome From Fluid Alone','**Small postoperative collections are common and may resolve without specific anti-inflammatory medication.** The presence of pericardial fluid does not establish PCIS or predict permanent constriction.',
    'Review the clinical course, inflammatory features, size and physiology. A new fever, pain, rising CRP or worsening collection changes the assessment and may justify investigation or treatment.',
    'Postoperative clot and regional tamponade can be important even without a large circumferential collection, but the supplied stable asymptomatic and well-assessed phenotype does not suggest that emergency.'),
  S('Separate Prevention Trials From Treatment of Established Fluid','**Colchicine prevention of an inflammatory postoperative syndrome does not prove that it clears an already established noninflammatory effusion.** The clinical indication and study population matter.',
    'A randomized trial of one hundred and ninety-seven patients with persistent moderate-to-large effusions seven to thirty days after surgery did not show reduced effusion volume or prevention of late tamponade with colchicine. That is a different question from COPPS-2 prophylaxis.',
    'Plan follow-up appropriate to size and the postoperative context, with earlier reassessment for dyspnea, presyncope, hypotension or new pain. Review antithrombotic treatment and other contributors through the cardiac surgical plan rather than making uncoordinated medication changes. Observation does not mean ignoring an enlarging or symptomatic collection. It means that neither inflammatory medication nor diagnostic puncture is automatically required for this specific reassuring presentation. Treat a later demonstrated cause or complication if it emerges instead of prescribing prophylactic steroids for a risk that the current findings do not establish.')],['POPE2','ESC2025','ACC2025'])

def highlight_values(text):
    text=re.sub(r'(?<![\w.])(?:>0\.34|≥0\.79|>1\.1)(?![\d.])',lambda m:'=='+m.group(0)+'==',text)
    return re.sub(r'(?<![\w.])(?:[<>≤≥]\s*)?\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:[–−-]\d+(?:\.\d+)?)?\s*(?:micrograms/kg/day|micrograms/day|micrograms|mg/m²|mg/kg/day|mg/kg|mg|mmHg|mm|ms|cm/s|U/L|mL|beats/min|%|°C)(?!\w)',lambda m:'=='+m.group(0)+'==',text)

if __name__=='__main__':
    decisions=[(t,d)for t,items in SECTIONS for d in items]
    assert len(rows)==len(decisions)
    for r,(t,d)in zip(rows,decisions):assert r['topic']==t;r['coverageDecision']=d
    for r in rows:
        r['sections']=[(h,[(highlight_values(t),[highlight_values(x)for x in kids])for t,kids in bs])for h,bs in r['sections']]
        if r['table']:
            headers,values=r['table'];r['table']=(headers,[[highlight_values(x)for x in line]for line in values])
    p=save('Cardiology',9,DOC,rows,R,'Original postgraduate educational synthesis with source checks and author editorial review. Full Harrison text not accessed; independent clinician review not claimed.')
    bank=json.loads(p.read_text(encoding='utf-8'));bank.update(status='chapter_authored',plannedQuestions=len(decisions));p.write_text(json.dumps(bank,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
    coverage=dict(subject='Cardiology',chapterNumber=9,chapter=DOC,status='coverage_reviewed',countPolicy='Coverage-driven distinct decisions; not official examination weights.',plannedQuestions=len(decisions),authoredQuestions=len(rows),sections=[dict(section=t,questions=len(items),decisions=items)for t,items in SECTIONS])
    coverage['questionDecisions']=[dict(question=i,title=r['title'],decision=r['coverageDecision'],section=r['topic'])for i,r in enumerate(rows,1)]
    (p.parent/(DOC+'.coverage.json')).write_text(json.dumps(coverage,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
    pp=ROOT/'progress.json';plan=json.loads(pp.read_text(encoding='utf-8'));entry=next(x for x in plan['chapters']if x['subject']=='Cardiology'and x['document']==DOC);entry.update(status='chapter_authored',authoredQuestions=len(rows),plannedQuestions=len(decisions));pp.write_text(json.dumps(plan,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
